\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Hungary CDS Spread Creeping Close To All-time Wides\b0\par\par\uc2 1,501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2011\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0903 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary CDS spread creeping close to all-time wides after the country's downgrade by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Ba1, non-investment grade late Thursday. Markit says Hungary's CDS spread is 24 bps wider at 622 bps, creeping close to its all-time wide of 635 bps in March 2009. Hungary's neighbors are also a tad weaker, with Czech Republic two bps wider at 190 bps and Poland three bps wider at 325 bps. (serena.ruffoni@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are mostly a tad lower with some foreigner names selling; trade is thin as some investors are cautious ahead of the BOT policy rate meeting on Wednesday. However, expectations of a larger rate cut by some local market participants are helping cap yields' upside, says a bond dealer. The "market is mainly focusing on the MPC's (Monetary Policy Committee) meeting, and most of them have already adjusted their positions in anticipation of a rate cut. Thus trade will likely remain thin until the meeting," he adds. Yields are expected to rise 2-3 bps on Monday. The bid/offer yield for bonds due December 2015 is quoted at 3.19%/3.15% from 3.17%/3.14% late Thursday, the June 2017 yield is unchanged from 3.32%/3.29% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.44%/3.41% from 3.43%/3.40%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] The downgrade by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  of Hungary's sovereign rating, to non-investment grade, is not expected to result in a disastrous outcome, according to Erste. The bank expects bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations in Friday's trading, but says the market response won't be extreme given that the pricing of Hungarian assets was clearly not reflecting investment grade for quite a while now. It adds that the downgrade may well prompt the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the 6% base rate at its upcoming rate-setting meeting. EUR/HUF trades at 316.16, from 309.52 late Thursday.(gergo.racz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary's cut is set to prompt a historic HUF low, Raiffeisen says, in the wake of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrading the country's sovereign rating to non-investment grade late Thursday. Raiffeisen expects HUF will respond by weakening against EUR, hitting historic lows as soon as Friday, exceeding the earlier record levels around 317. It adds the conditions will likely also compel the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike the base rate to 6.25% from the current 6.00% at its upcoming rate-setting meeting Tuesday. EUR/HUF is at 316.12 vs 309.52 late Thursday. (gergo.racz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  is long TRY/HUF and PLN/HUF after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgraded Hungary to junk status. "Everyone will now be watching S&P. We believe that the rating agency will announce its decision within the coming week. We expected eventual downgrades from the two rating agencies but it is interesting that \uc2 Moody's\uc2  decided to go ahead in spite of the recent news that Hungary would seek support from the IMF and EU," says the bank. EUR/HUF trades at 316.47, while EUR/PLN trades at 4.505. (alexandra.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is higher as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected of buying dollars at 30.430, say two local traders. The pair is at 30.460 from its intraday low of 30.412 before the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with 30.442 late Thursday in Taipei. Hectic buying from foreign investors lifted the pair to its intraday high of 30.505 in the morning, but it turned weaker in the afternoon as some foreign investors started to square their long positions while exporters' selling also intensified, they say. They tip the pair in a 30.400-30.500 band Monday. But one trader says, "if local stocks keep falling sharply next week, the greenback is likely to advance above 30.600 on foreign fund outflows." A second trader adds, the pair is also more likely to track the rising USD/KRW in the near term. Taiwan shares end down 1.2%, with foreign investors net sellers of NT$9.12 billion worth of local shares. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] India's Sensex is off 0.2% at 15,829.80 in choppy trading, as market participants refrain from taking fresh positions ahead of the weekend, an analyst says. A credit rating downgrade in Hungary and weak consumer confidence data from France further dims the mood, he adds. "The sentiment remains delicate, with investors gradually turning away from the stock markets," the analyst says, adding "the euro-zone debt crisis contagion appears more real now and the problem is going to linger for a fairly long time." The problem is compounded with the domestic economy facing headwinds from high inflation and a weak INR. \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 is down 1.6% at INR2,623.10 and \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) is off 1.0% at INR766.55. \uc2 Larsen & Toubro\uc2  (500510.BY) is down 4.6% at INR1,281.00. Retail stocks remain buoyed after the cabinet approved easing foreign-investment rules in the sector. \uc2 Pantaloon Retail\uc2  India (523574.BY) is up 17.8% at INR236.30. The analyst tips the Sensex in a 15,660-15,910 range for the session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are down as stocks move off their lows, although trading volumes remain light. Gilts have managed to outperform bunds smartly in recent sessions, with the 10-year UK benchmark yield trading almost flat to its German counterpart on Thursday, according to Tradeweb. The five-year yield is still below the yield on German bobls. The data calendar is empty so gilts should continue to take cues from moves in euro-zone bond markets and equities. December gilts are down 0.17, and the 10-year cash yield is at 2.18%, up 2 bps. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP hit a new 8-week high late Friday, lifted by continuing concerns over Europe's debt crisis. The pair is at 43.94, its highest level since October 4 vs 43.66 late Thursday. Volume is heavy. If the 44.00 level is breached, traders expect the USD/PHP to meet resistance at 44.20 while support is at 43.80. A local bank trader says while the PHP may get a boost Monday if 3Q gross domestic product data surprises on the upside, "the main concern is still Europe. If more bad news comes out of Europe during the weekend, expect the dollar to head to 44.20." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  is betting on the fact that a recession in the euro zone will spill over to Norway and Sweden and will undermine both Nordic countries' "otherwise stellar fiscal profiles." It sells an equally-weighted basket of NOK and SEK against EUR set at 100 and looks for a 5% return with a stop 2.5% below. Also opens a tactical short AUD/NZD trade at 1.3120 and targeting a move towards 1.26 and a stop above 1.33 on a New York close basis. Although the bank likes AUD/NZD higher, it sees the pair as overvalued relative to current pricing of monetary policy. AUD/NZD now at 1.3086. (jessica.mead@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] Avoid CEE \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  or go short, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , after Hungary's downgrade. "[Go] short the Polish zloty, Hungarian forint and the somewhat illiquid Romania leu... other EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be negative impacted by [euro area bank] deleveraging, but the yield pickup and valuation metrics offer compensation," says the bank. EUR/HUF trades at 316.27. (alexandra.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] ING says EUR/HUF may break above 320 although there is a risk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or raise rates as early as today to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ING says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of the country to junk status is troubling as the country needs to raise EUR4 billion in financing next year and most of Hungarian local debt is owned by foreign banks. "As was the case in 2008, PLN liquidity will see the PLN sold as a proxy hedge for HUF," bank says, adding that EUR/PLN could push up to 4.5750-4.6000, but more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very likely. EUR/HUF at 316.42, EUR/PLN at 4.5079. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 25, 2011 04:03 ET (09:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111125e7bp001y3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
