\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-UPDATE 1-Hungary cbank steps in to prop up weak forint\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2011\par 15:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects size of t-bill sale in paragraph 6)\par \par \uc2 * C.bank says forint falls not in line with fundamentals\par \par \uc2 * Says "gradual tightening" of monetary conditions may be needed\par \par \uc2 * Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives scant immediate support to forint\par \par \uc2 * Gradual rate hikes may come if forint fails to recover -analyst\par \par \uc2 By Gergely Szakacs and Sandor Peto\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Nov 15 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally on Tuesday to prop up the weak forint, which fell to record lows to the euro late on Monday, and said a gradual tightening of monetary conditions may be needed if risk aversion in European markets is lasting.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian five-year credit default swaps traded above 600 basis points, close to their March 2009 record high after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  and Fitch both put Hungary on negative credit watch on Friday, increasing the chances of a cut to "junk" rating status.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold its next regular policy meeting on Nov. 29 after keeping rates unchanged at 6 percent last month .\par \par \uc2 "If the increase in risk aversion affecting European financial markets proves lasting, a gradual tightening of monetary conditions may be necessary," the rate-setting Monetary Council said after a regular non-rate meeting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's forint, the weakest performer in central Europe this year, sank to a fresh record low at 317.9 to the euro on Monday after the two rating agencies warned Budapest it may lose its investment-grade status.\par \par \uc2 Poor demand also forced the debt agency to scrap a six-week bill auction on Monday. It sold 40 billion forints of three-month bills on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The market falls prompted the Monetary Council to issue a statement saying the forint's recent decline was unjustified.\par \par \uc2 "In the Monetary Council's assessment, the recent depreciation of the forint is not in line with the fundamentals of the Hungarian economy," the panel said, adding that forint falls also worsened the inflation outlook.\par \par \uc2 The Monetary Council said the government's commitment to bringing the budget deficit below 3 percent of economic output next year and efforts to reduce debt meant a spillover of fears about debt sustainability from the euro zone was misplaced.\par \par \uc2 The comments provided scant relief for the forint, which traded at 314.75 versus the euro at 1337 GMT, a shade stronger than 314.90 before the bank's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One analyst said gradual rate rises could be on the cards if the forint fails to recover meaningfully.\par \par \uc2 "I agree that the market falls are caused by speculation rather than fundamental factors. A one-off big interest rate hike is not the solution," said analyst Zsolt Kondrat at \uc2 MKB Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "They must say what they just said and if the exchange rate does not rebound, they must start to lift interest rates gradually, saying that these exchange rate levels already carry inflation risks," he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Andras Simor had warned after the October policy meeting, when the forint traded at stronger levels of around 300 to the euro, that the weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could push meeting the bank's inflation target further into 2013.\par \par \uc2 Inflation will rise next year on the back of government tax hikes to rein in Hungary's budget deficit and keep European Union development funds flowing in at a time when the economy heads into a period of near stagnation.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Gergely Szakacs, Sandor Peto; Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY-CBANK/  (UPDATE 1) (CORRECTED)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111118e7bf0010y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
