\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Zloty eases on caution ahead of EU summit\b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2011\par 12:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regaining some ground, bonds)\par \par \uc2 * Zloty erases previous session gains on euro angst\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  still off recent lows, \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts longer-term debt sales\par \par \uc2 * Stocks also drop as expectations fade for euro summit\par \par \uc2 By Sam Cage\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, Oct 20 (Reuters) - Poland's zloty erased the previous day's gains on Thursday as traders took defensive positions ahead of a key European summit, while fears of a ratings cut hit a Hungarian debt tender.\par \par \uc2 European policymakers are struggling to reach agreement on how to address the debt crisis, and the euro and stocks fell on French President Nicolas Sarkozy's comments that Paris and Berlin were at odds before the weekend meeting.\par \par \uc2 Events in the euro zone are dominating central European markets because of their close trade and banking ties, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  regained some ground as the euro rallied on clearer signs of how European officials plan to tackle the debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Failure to come up with a plan brought back risk-off," a Bucharest dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Polish and Romanian central banks have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping to move them away from 27-month and 15-month lows respectively. Dealers said while \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were still not far from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  territory, action seemed unlikely unless losses accelerated.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that CEE \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be unable to develop any significant appreciation beyond short spells of central banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," BNP analysts said in a note.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold less 5- and 15-year debt than it had originally planned to as fears of a ratings downgrade and euro zone turmoil reduced demand for longer paper. It sold 20 billion forints ($93 million) worth of 3-year bonds as planned.\par \par \uc2 "Some in the market are afraid of a ratings downgrade, some fear an emergency rate hike, and there are those who think the fears are unjustified," a Budapest-based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Concerns Hungary may lose its investment-grade rating also pressured the forint in early trade before it recouped early losses to trade 0.3 percent higher on the day.\par \par \uc2 ROMANIA YIELDS RISING\par \par \uc2 The zloty shed 0.3 percent, erasing all of the previous day's gains, and the Czech crown -- the region's best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year -- was flat at 24.877 per euro.\par \par \uc2 A Prague dealer said the crown will again test its September low of 25.015 and if it breaks that, it could fall towards previous lows hit in January.\par \par \uc2 The region's stock markets were all in the red, also led by Poland, where the Warsaw bourse shed more than 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Romania's leu was a touch higher at 4.33 per euro and markets will eye a tender for 600 million lei in three-year bonds, expecting the finance ministry will have to stump up higher yields than 7.49 percent when it last sold similar paper, or sell less than planned.\par \par \uc2 "I think there will more or less be a 4.33-4.35 interval today, on hold for the weekend events, provided the Germans and French don't express more disagreements in the meantime," said 