\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Inflation eases in CEE, rate paths may diverge\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2011\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Hungary CPI at 3.6 pct y/y flat from Aug, cbank's hands tied\par \par \uc2 * Romania's inflation drops sharply, cbank may have room to cut\par \par \uc2 * Czech inflation falls 0.2 pct m/m in Sept\par \par \uc2 By Krisztina Than\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Oct 11 (Reuters) - Lower food prices helped keep a lid on inflation in Romania and Hungary last month and inflation pressures remain muted across the EU's eastern wing, but some central banks may still have to hold or even raise interest rates to counter growing market risks.\par \par \uc2 In Romania inflation slowed sharply to 3.5 percent in September -- the lowest annual rate since the 1989 fall of communism -- entering the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target band and indicating it might have room to start cutting rates soon.\par \par \uc2 Romania's borrowing costs are the highest in central Europe at 6.25 percent but its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past month and analysts say that has stayed its hand on cutting rates.\par \par \uc2 In neighbouring Hungary, inflation at 3.6 percent in September also came in slightly below analysts' forecast for 3.7 percent, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  there has no room for manoeuvre at all due to a recent surge in the country's risk premia and the forint's weakening .\par \par \uc2 "We see a relatively significant easing of inflation going forward and countries like Poland and the Czech Republic clearly have room for monetary easing," said Lars Christensen at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The story is slightly different in Romania and Hungary."\par \par \uc2 In the Czech Republic consumer prices fell 0.2 percent in September on a month-on-month basis, putting the annual inflation rate at 1.8 percent, which analysts said would allow the bank to keep rates stable at a record low of 0.75 percent or even consider a cut down the line.\par \par \uc2 The minutes of the last rate meeting there showed on Sept. 30 that the bulk of the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board believed risks to the bank's inflation outlook were slightly on the downside.\par \par \uc2 September inflation data for Poland, by far the region's largest economy, is due on Oct. 13.\par \par \uc2 Minutes from the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest meeting showed that the balance of risks for inflation had become more even but did not point to a need for rate cuts. The bank has also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the zloty.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HUNGARY\b0 \highlight\uc2  HANDS TIED\par \par \uc2 With the global growth slowing and markets worried that the euro zone's debt problems will spread into a full scale banking crisis to rival that in 2008, policymakers across the region's developing markets would like to do more to bolster growth.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's government will hike its top value added tax rate to 27 percent from 25 percent from January, raising inflation. But inflation pressures from the demand side continue to be extremely low, and the economy is slowing -- with some analysts now forecasting zero growth next year or even a recession.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose base interest rates stand at 6 percent , has said once the effects of the tax hikes wear off inflation could ease back to its 3 percent target by the beginning of 2013.\par \par \uc2 But the forint is trading close to 300 versus the euro\par \par \uc2 and Hungary's 5-year credit default swaps are at around 524 basis points, near their highest levels since March 2009.\par \par \uc2 "In the next 3 months inflation is expected to remain between 3.5-4.0 percent and then jumping above 4 percent in January due to the VAT-hike," said Zsolt Kondrat at \uc2 MKB Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot reduce interest rates primarily due to financial stability risks, and secondly because it will wait to see potential second-round effects from next year's tax hikes on inflationary expectations," added Gergely Suppan at Takarekbank.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Nordea Bank\uc2  said the Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would face a similar dilemma as Romanian households also have a large amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans and the country has a high proportion of Greek and Italian banks operating there.\par \par \uc2 "Although the improved inflation outlook and subdued domestic demand would argue for a rate cut, it is difficult with the markets' risk perception of Romania," it said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Krisztina Than)\par \par \uc2 EASTEUROPE-INFLATION/ (WRAPUP)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111011e7ab000hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
