\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Hungary would like higher forint but can manage-minister\b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2011\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * First government comment on weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after recent falls\par \par \uc2 * Would be more satisfied with 268 EUR/HUF level -state sec\par \par \uc2 * Says 2012 budget reserves can handle exchange rate changes\par \par \uc2 * Even more important is less exchange rate volatility -state sec\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Oct 5 (Reuters) - (Adds further details, comments)\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Oct 5 (Reuters) - Hungary's government would be happier with forint levels of around 268 to the euro, but the 2012 draft budget has large reserves to handle exchange rate risks from a weaker forint, a top Economy Ministry official said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The remarks by State Secretary Zoltan Csefalvay are the first comments to made by the Hungarian government on the weakness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after recent heavy falls which sent the forint to 29-month lows at 300 to the euro this week.\par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government stepped in to prop up the ailing zloty in the past two weeks and dealers say the Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also be supporting the leu through market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched weekly euro tenders for commercial banks this week to supply lenders with liquidity and temper exchange rate volatility as foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debtors repay their loans at discounted rates under a government scheme.\par \par \uc2 "It would not be good if the forint was stuck at levels around 300 (to the euro)," state secretary Zoltan Csefalvay was quoted as saying by private broadcaster Gazdasagi Radio on its website when asked about the impact of the euro's strength on the 2012 budget, which was built on an assumed 268 euro/forint exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "But next year's budget contains a fund worth 300 billion forints ($1.33 billion), or slightly above 1 percent of GDP, which for the most part can be devoted to this end, if the exchange rate changes," he said.\par \par \uc2 The forint sank to fresh 29-month lows of 300.65 against the euro on Wednesday, pressured by growing fears over the euro zone debt crisis and a controversial Hungarian foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans repayment scheme, which compounds global risks.\par \par \uc2 The government submitted the 2012 budget draft to parliament on Friday, targeting a deficit of 2.5 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the government had an exchange rate target, Csefalvay said:\par \par \uc2 "We have already touched upon what next year's budget reckons with (268 to the euro), we would be satisfied with that," he said.\par \par \uc2 "But what is even more important, an even bigger issue is the strong volatility, which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debtors can feel day by day, but obviously the Hungarian economy also feels this strong volatility."\par \par \uc2 He said a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help make Hungarian exports more competitive, adding however, that genuine competitiveness could be achieved only if goods and services in Hungary can be marketed even at a stronger forint exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 ($1=224.92 Hungarian forints)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Gergely Szakacs; Editing by Greg Mahlich)\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY-FORINT/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111008e7a5000e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
