\par\par \b \uc2 DJ FOCUS: Hungary Could Be Next To Join New  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  War\b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 15:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Forint at critical level that could spur \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on spot market first, with rate hikes seen as last resort \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Clare Connaghan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--With the forint sinking fast, Hungary could become the latest emerging-market country in Eastern Europe and Asia to try to prop up its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the wake of mounting concerns over Greece and the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hungarian forint sunk Tuesday to its weakest level against the euro since April 21, 2009, having broken the critically important HUF300 level, which market participants anticipate could trigger action from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Hungary is more sensitive than most to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness, because it makes it much more expensive for Hungarian households to pay back the foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mortgages that the majority of them hold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the forint under increased pressure, it might be "time to pull the emergency brake," said Nordea economist Elisabeth Andreew, adding that the possibility of emergency interest rate raises is also increasing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Back in October 2008, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised interest rates by 300 basis points to support the forint. While such aggressive monetary action can't be ruled out this time around, Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to try other options first, market participants said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The economy is very weak and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may resort to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first," said Murat Toprak, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hungarian forint--like other emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia, Latin America, Eastern Europe and Africa--have been under heavy selling pressure in recent weeks as a toxic combination of fears about the global economy and escalating euro-zone debt worries lead investors to fold their bets in emerging markets on a colossal scale.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars of the recent past have metamorphosed, with authorities in emerging markets this time embarking on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -strengthening programs, having previously battled to hold their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down to keep a competitive edge.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Authorities in Poland, Romania and Russia have all stepped in to defend their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent days, while Turkey's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holds daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  auctions to stem lira weakness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Countries across Asia have also been very active in defending their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with South Korean, Malaysian, Indonesian, and Philippine central banks all suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sudden \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness can pose inflationary problems by lifting the cost of imported goods, and for those countries burdened with large amounts of debt denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it is particularly harmful because these debts become much more expensive to pay back.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Clare Connaghan, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0) 20 7842 9496; clare.connaghan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 04, 2011 09:28 ET (13:28 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111004e7a4003mg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
