\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Hungary Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Declines Comment On Euro  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2011\par 13:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 -- Traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a rumor\par \par \uc2 -- Raiffeisen said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep EUR/HUF below 275\par \par \uc2 (Adds traders' comments in paragraphs five to 11, background in paragraphs six and 12.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Veronika Gulyas and Gergo Racz \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--The Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday declined to comment on talk that it would buy the Hungarian forint against the euro to keep the exchange rate below HUF275, while traders said there had been no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's response came after a Raiffeisen Bank analyst said the bank was attempting to keep the euro-forint cross at bay amid a nervous market environment and a surging Swiss franc, which strengthened to an all-time high of HUF273 early Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't comment on foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves and it never commented in the past either," the bank told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other market analysts dismissed the idea that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at current, relatively benign euro-forint levels. At 1150 GMT, the euro was trading at HUF272.97 from around HUF278 Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but it has no reason [to do so] looking at the current forint-Swiss franc exchange rate," said Equilor analyst Szilard Buro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders contacted by Dow Jones Newswires were unanimous that there had been no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  trader said such a move was highly unlikely since events are driven by global developments and not happenings in Hungary's economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite disappointing industrial output and foreign trade data suggesting that the country's growth is likely to slow earlier than predicted, the government is sticking to its strict fiscal policy and has pledged to keep the budget deficit below 3% of gross domestic product in 2011 and future years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forint hasn't yet reached a level against the euro which would warrant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Peter Attard Montalto of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura International\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note to analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Above HUF280 we'll see the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  start to verbally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , around HUF290 they'll be in the market more aggressively first on the quiet and then making a public splash, around HUF300 they will raise rates," Attard Montalto said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A trader also said the forint is holding stable against the euro, therefore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely. Furthermore, it would actually make the forint vulnerable as it would make the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appear weak and a possible subject for speculation.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reportedly last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2009 when the euro reached an all-time high of HUF317.10 against the forint March 6. At the time, traders and analysts had mixed opinions as to whether there was indeed any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the bank never acknowledged that had done so.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Gergo Racz and Veronika Gulyas, Dow Jones Newswires, +361 267 0623; veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com [ 10-08-11 1158GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110810e78a001bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
