\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Hungary Monetary Policy Fears To Calm-Raiffeisen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] Market fears about direct political \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy should get "less pronounced" following the government's two nominations to the Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Raiffeisen says. "The two candidates have a relatively low profile... their professional background is certainly positive." As a result, he expects the key interest rate to be kept on hold in the next 6 months. "We are confident that personnel changes in the monetary council should not change the stance of monetary policy." (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] India's weak current account and smaller capital inflows compared with other Asian emerging markets may make the INR one of the underperformers in the region, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in a note. "That said, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to be underpinned by ongoing dollar weakness this week, a factor we think points to a modest move lower in USD/INR," house says. The house tips the pair in a 44.85-45.25 range for the week. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is back in demand after its early selloff sparked by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of Greece. EUR/USD found demand above 1.3950 and has just broken to a fresh post-payroll high of 1.4018. Offers are touted around 1.4920 and 1.4950. EUR/CHF has added half a cent to 1.2980, EUR/JPY is back above 115.00 however EUR/GBP is underperforming and at 0.8588 is well off the day's 0.8607 highs. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are steady in heavy volume; offshore players are buying long-dated notes with maturity of over 10 years but supply is on par with demand, says a bond dealer at a local bank. Investors are treading carefully ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy rate meeting Wednesday; though most players expect the BOT to hike the rate by 25 bps. Yields are expected to fall by two-three bps Tuesday. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.41%/3.35% from 3.40%/3.39% in late Asian trade Friday, the October 2017 yield is bid at 3.62%/3.50% from 3.62%/3.54% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.95%/3.92% from 3.95%/3.93%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end 0.5% higher at 3561.717, off an intraday low of 3543.006, as hopes for strong 2010 earnings for many banks attracted demand, traders say. They expect the market to continue to gain Tuesday on further foreign buying, tipping the main index to test the psychological level of 3600. "Expectations that many companies will distribute higher 2010 dividend per shares should also help inspire buying," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Foreigners are net buyers of IDR585 billion worth of shares. Among gainers are \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), up 2.4% at IDR6,350, BCA (BBCA.JK) is +1.5% at IDR6,900, while cigarette maker \uc2 Gudang Garam\uc2  (GGRM.JK) is +4.4% at IDR33,750. Profit taking hits coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK), which is down 1.7% at IDR2,975. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] India's lack of government bond auctions and an increase in bank deposits before the quarter-end may support the country's bonds over the next month, says a \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  note. Banks have to invest 24% of their net deposits in government bonds, and rising deposits translate into banks holding more sovereign paper. The house tips the benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond yield may stay around 7.90% in the near-term, compared with the current 7.95%. "However, we are likely to see the 10-year yield moving toward 8.40% in FY12 on supply pressures. Even with the lower fiscal deficit target, we expect INR4.83 trillion of net securities to be issued in FY12, compared with our estimate of demand of INR4.43 trillion, which implies about INR400 billion of excess supply." The house expects, upward pressure on rates will be more acute in fiscal 1H12 when the government is set to issue 65% of the fiscal year's bond supply, but with the banks set to see only 40% of the full-year deposit growth. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 GMT [Dow Jones] ING believes USD/PHP could retreat further on prospects of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate hike, although concerns over rising oil price may slow that slide and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair within the initial technical trading range of 43.25-43.60. "But the lower end of the trading range could be at risk in the coming weeks," says ING. It adds, the surprisingly high 4.3% inflation in February raises the specter of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifting policy rates later this month by as much as 50 bps. ING moved up the timing of the first rate hike to 2Q instead of 4Q, and pencils a 50 bp increase plus 25 bps each in the 3Q and 4Q. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] Shorter-end Dutch state loan paper has cheapened to attractive levels against their direct core-country peers in recent weeks, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . They recommend switching out of Finnish RFGB September 2014 into the DSL January 2015 to pick up around 20bps, and they see good value in switching out of French BTAN January 2014s into DSL January 2014s at a 1bp give-up. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of England is likely to follow the ECB in raising interest rates to temper inflation, with the latter having already expressed a clear intent to tighten policy, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's economists expect the BOE to raise the base rate by 125bps between now and 2Q 2012. In contrast, the Fed is unlikely to tighten until later in 2012. For investors looking to fade the recent flight to quality driven drop in U.K. yields, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  recommends paying 5-year UK swaps vs US, in anticipation of a further medium-term widening of this spread by around 60bps. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end 0.5% lower at 1515.74 as the rise in oil prices and continued political crisis in Middle East keep sentiment cautious, a local trader says. He also says investors are choosing to book profits at every rally due to the prevailing uncertainties. "Volumes are substantially below normal, which suggests many players are just choosing to stay out." OSK Research says the psychological 1,500 mark would represent the next support for the market. Market breadth is negative with losers trumping gainers by over three to one. Malaysian Airline (3786.KU) ends down 3.2% at MYR1.81, Hong Leong Financial (1082.KU) is down 1.3% at MYR8.81, \uc2 AMMB Holdings\uc2  (1015.KU) is down 1.3% at MYR6.30 and \uc2 Telekom Malaysia\uc2  (4863.KU) is down 1.2% at MYR3.99. Among gainers, \uc2 Petronas Dagangan\uc2  (5681.KU) is up 0.7% at MYR14.30 and \uc2 Petronas Chemicals Group\uc2  (5183.KU) is up 0.2% at MYR6.34. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] While there has been discussion about potential planting changes in China, as farmers earnings should double per year by a shift to planting cotton instead of corn and wheat, cotton will not necessarily see ample supply, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says in a research note. The house says corn and wheat prices have quickly caught up to cotton, which is up by more than 180% since 2010, and grains and cotton are fighting for planting resources as the revenue gap narrows. The house says this could be good news for upstream players such as Sateri (1768.HK)and Texhong Textile (2678.HK), with exposure to dissolving wood pulp and cotton yarns, respectively. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  sets Sateri's target price at HK$10.55 and Texhong's target at HK$9.50, rating both at Outperform. Texhong ends down 1.2% at HK$8.16 and Sateri is down 1.2% at HK$7.52. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY ran into aggressive selling Friday when it spiked to 83.00 and this price action warns of a temporary pullback says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . However, in the absence of a break back below 81.35 the bank says it would look to buy such a dip for a push toward 84.00, although a break below 81.10 would give reason to abandon this bullish scenario. USD/JPY now at 82.09. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) [ 03-07-11 0431ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110307e7370005w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
