\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE: IMF Breakdown Whacks Hungarian Forint, Debt Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2010\par 13:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds background on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, analyst comment, and updates levels.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Katie Martin \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets have slipped sharply after the country failed over the weekend to reach a funding deal with the International Monetary Fund and the European Union.\par \par \uc2 The euro shot almost 3% higher against the forint at the start of European trading hours to reach just over HUF290, taking the forint back down to its lowest levels in over a year, as Hungary failed to deliver the austerity measures required for it to receive the remainder of its existing EUR20 billion standby credit line.\par \par \uc2 The cost of insuring Hungarian government bonds against default rocketed higher, with five-year credit default swaps quoted 43 basis points wider at 365 basis points. That means it now costs $365,000 a year to insure $10 million of such debt against a failure to repay.\par \par \uc2 The forint and CDS levels are now back at the weak points they reached last month, when Hungarian officials warned that the country may be heading towards a Greek-style debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The country's key stock market index also declined 4% at the open Monday, with shares in Hungary's biggest bank, OTP, shedding some 8.5% of their value. Prices later sprang back a little, indicating that early panic-style trading was moderating.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets offered a sign that the blow to Hungarian assets was sending ripples throughout the region, as the Polish zloty and Czech koruna also weakened, though declines for these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were much more modest.\par \par \uc2 Further losses are seen ahead for Hungarian markets, with analysts predicting that the breakdown in IMF/EU talks will hit investors' confidence in Hungary for the long term.\par \par \uc2 "The market has drawn a clear conclusion: without additional IMF and EU support, Hungary will be hard pressed not to default," said analysts at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in Frankfurt in a note to clients. "In this situation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will doubtless not cut its key rates today, even though the government wants it to."\par \par \uc2 Further sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to inject some stability, several analysts suggested. A weak forint brings headaches for Hungary by ramping up repayment costs on outstanding foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated loans.\par \par \uc2 "There is the very clear risk that further weakness in the forint will prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Neil Mellor, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon in London. "The market is clearly fully aware of this probability, with the upward spike in the euro today coming to a dramatic halt just south of the June high of HUF290.30."\par \par \uc2 The breakdown in talks between Hungary and the multilateral bodies has implications beyond Eastern Europe, as it offers a clear sign that international aid is tied to strict conditions.\par \par \uc2 "Hungary said it was already in the fifth year of austerity measures and it was not possible to tighten the screws any further. That's critical because it's exactly the problem the euro zone faces; austerity measures... are not one-offs, they are multiyear projects," said Simon Penn, an analyst at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "The IMF and EU have not fudged a solution. Hungary won't play ball so the funding line is suspended," he added. "Remember, Greece's fiscal position is far worse than Hungary's."\par \par \uc2 At 1105 GMT, the forint was still wedged at its lower levels, with the euro trading at HUF288.02. The Polish zloty and Czech koruna had bounced back somewhat, with the euro trading at CZK25.462 from a peak of CZK25.517 and at PLN4.1168 from a peak of PLN4.1548.\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Margit Feher in Budapest and Michael Wilson in London contributed to this article.) [ 19-07-10 1124GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100719e67j000ue\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
