\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 5- \highlight22\uc2 Hungary \highlight\uc2  assets dive, cbank threatens  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,067 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2010\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Forint, stx, bonds hammered; cbank threatens \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Other CEE assets gain back some morning losses\par \par \uc2 * Romania sells less T-bills than planned as expected\par \par \uc2 (Updates with \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  cbank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments)\par \par \uc2 By Marius Zaharia\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, July 19 (Reuters) - Hungarian assets plunged on Monday following the suspension of IMF/EU funding talks over the weekend as markets shrugged off \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warnings it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left borrowing costs unchanged at 5.25 percent on Monday but said it would resume transfers of EU funds in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to fight forint volatility, adding that higher interest rates may also be needed. [ID:nBUS002150]\par \par \uc2 The forint <EURHUF=> was untouched by the comments, and traded 3.1 percent down on the day at 290.46 per euro at 1341 GMT, even weaker than levels seen before them.\par \par \uc2 "While these comments and actions might limit speculative pressure against the forint, it doesn't provide much relief against the external funding pressures ... or shore up the government's credibility during the current impasse with the IMF," said Koon Chow of \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hungarian bond yields rose 20-30 basis points across the curve on Monday, while Budapest stocks <.BUX> fell 2.3 percent. Yields on Hungary's Eurobond maturing June 2011 <HU013159386=RRPS> shot up to their highest since June 2009, while five-year CDS spreads rose 58 basis points, according to Markit.\par \par \uc2 Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked the forint's decline in overnight trade, but later recovered.\par \par \uc2 Dealers say central European assets are decoupling from Hungary's problems as Poland and the Czech Republic have sounder fundamentals than Hungary and Romania, while the latter has overcome significant hurdles to keep its own IMF deal on-track.\par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty <EURPLN=> was down 0.8 percent, trading at 4.14 per euro, significantly off overnight lows of 4.1540. The Romanian leu <EURRON=> was a touch lower, while the Czech crown <EURCZK=>, seen as a regional safe haven, was 0.4 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 In Romania, the finance ministry stuck to its self-imposed cut-off yield of 7 percent -- half of 2009 peaks -- and sold roughly a fifth of what it planned at a one-year T-bill auction. [ID:nBUC003742]\par \par \uc2 Such a result was widely expected, since the ministry has either rejected all bids or cut tenders across maturities since early May, when investors began demanding higher yields in response to worries over the success of the government's austerity drive.\par \par \uc2 As an alternative way to seek funding, the ministry said on Monday it plans to sell one-year euro-denominated paper on local markets worth 400 million euros. [ID:nBUC003743]\par \par \uc2 In Poland, June industrial output data came in significantly better than expected, highlighting the country's economic outperformance in the region. [ID:nWSF009293]\par \par \uc2 MARKET PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Gyorgy Matolcsy said he still expected to reach an agreement with the International Monetary Fund and the European Union as talks may resume in September, but markets face uncertainty until then. [ID:nLDE66I0VT]\par \par \uc2 Some economists said the forint may test levels close to 300 per euro before the government decides to resume negotiations, but many say it is to early to assess how far the sell-off would go, especially with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its toes.\par \par \uc2 Hungary faces no immediate pressure on state finances and its 2010 position looks safe, but economists agree it needs to keep the IMF deal on track as an external anchor of credibility.\par \par \uc2 An immediate risk from the suspension of IMF/EU talks was depreciation against the Swiss franc, also seen in the zloty.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation against the euro may have helped Hungary to tame a recession and Poland to avoid one, weakening against the Swiss franc has caused big pain for these countries as a large chunk of household loans taken when their economies were booming are denominated in francs.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the forint fell more than 3 percent against the Swiss franc <CHFHUF=> from Friday to 211.6, while the zloty was quoted 0.6 percent weaker at 3.0362 <CHFPLN=>.\par \par \uc2 Cheuvreux noted the forint was now around 38 percent weaker than levels at which many mortgage loans were taken. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                                            today    in 2010 \par Czech crown      <EURCZK=>  25.34   25.432   +0.36%    +3.86%\par Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>   4.14    4.106   -0.82%    -0.87%\par Hungarian forint <EURHUF=> 290.46  281.54    -3.07%    -6.92%\par Croatian kuna    <EURHRK=>   7.213   7.225   +0.17%    +1.33%\par Romanian leu     <EURRON=> 