\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Heading To 89.00 - Mizuho\b0\par\par\uc2 1,233 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2010\par 12:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY had its lowest daily close since May and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Nicole Elliott says despite the dollar being slightly oversold she looks for more slippage and a test of 89.00 later this week. Now at 89.55 she favors shorts from 89.80, adding at 91.00 with a stop above 91.50, targeting 89.50 ahead of 89.00. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1022 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary sells less 12-month Treasury bills than planned as market sentiment turns negative after Fitch Ratings requires further fiscal tightening in Greece, a trader says. A further factor depressing bids is that the Hungarian market is gradually pricing in the end of the current interest rate easing cycle, the trader adds. Hungary's Debt Management Agency sold HUF40Bln ($175M) of 12-month T-bills versus its HUF50Bln target as bids totalled only HUF57.50 billion. The big difference between the minimum yield of 5.22%, average yield of 5.41% and maximum yield of 5.50% signal weak bids. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1009 GMT [Dow Jones] The outlook for SEK continues to improve, says SEB. "We expect a rate hike by the Riksbank in July which would further support SEK." It also notes the strong Swedish manufacturing confidence released Wednesday "makes our EUR/SEK model point even lower than before [now 9.12]. EUR/SEK trades at 9.5735. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday unexpectedly hikes its benchmark interest rate by 12.5bps, first rate hike since June 2008. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to curb the (overheating) housing market," says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Cheng Cheng-mount. Adds, rates hike decision suggests "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is optimistic over island's economic recovery." \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says in statement economy performs better-than-expected with strong exports, investment expansion. Cheng expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue hiking benchmark rates in 3Q. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1001 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end down 1.7% at day's low of 793.19 in active trade as weak European markets, U.S. stock futures drag in late hours. Selling accelerated after SET Index slips below 800 psychological level, says Phillip Securities' Assistant VP Teerada Chanyingyong. "800 is the resistance level now." Next support eyed at 783. Among top actives, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) down 1.5% at THB19.80, PTT (PTT.TH) down 1.6% at THB250, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 1.6% at THB624, \uc2 Thanachart Capital\uc2  (TCAP.TH) down 3.4% at THB28.25. Bucking trend, \uc2 Sri Trang Agro Industry\uc2  (STA.TH) +6.2% at THB90.25 on higher rubber price. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end down 0.4% at 2914.095 in thin volume on profit-taking, ending 3-session winning streak, dealers say. "We still see the composite index in a bullish trend in medium to long term," says analyst at Panin Sekuritas; tips 2,887-2,930 band tomorrow. Among gainers, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +2.7% at IDR5,800, Bukit Asam +1.8% at IDR17,350. Losers are Bank Central Asia (BBCA.JK), down 2.6% at IDR5,650, Bumi (BUMI.JK) down 1.1% at IDR1,890.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0952 GMT [Dow Jones] European corporate credit markets are wider Thursday after a tighter opening, moving in tandem with falling stocks in Europe amid a very quiet market, traders say. The Markit iTraxx Crossover index is around 4 bps basis points wider at 547/551 bps, while the iTraxx Europe is about 2 bps basis points wider at 126/127 bps. However, pressures continue to stem from the sovereigns. "There also does seem to be a desire to reduce exposure to (non-core European countries)--especially from market makers--ahead of quarter-end. This is another negative for liquidity in what was once an uber liquid asset class," says Jim Reid at Deutche Bank. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD trades back above 1.50 for the first time in six weeks, while EUR/GBP drops to a fresh 19 month low of 0.8185. Sterling is back in favor after Tuesday's well-received emergency budget and the appearance of a hawk at the MPC. GBP/USD is now at 1.5010, with Fibonacci resistance coming in at 1.5022 and 1.5065. Next EUR/GBP support is at 0.8169, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's Karen Jones. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0933 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB rises to 32.42 vs 32.37-32.375 late yesterday in tandem with general firmer tone of USD/Regionals. Possible portfolio outflows amid falling local stocks also bolstering. Gains limited by exporters' sale at just above 32.40 level. "We have seen some active dollar buying by some Japanese names and some other onshore foreign banks. With this momentum rolling, we may see the dollar/baht continues to edge up further tomorrow," says Bangkok-based dealer. Immediate resistance at 32.45, then 32.50 while support pegged at 32.35. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds edge up, propelled by more active buying in afternoon trade mainly by offshore players. "The situation has become favorable for bond market," says local dealer; adds Fed keeping interest rates record low, spike in Greece's credit default swap, sharp slides in European, local markets stoke bond demand. Yields expected to fall by up to 5 bps Friday, Bid/offer yields of issue due May 2015 quoted at 2.92%/2.91% vs 2.975%/2.965% late yesterday, November 2016 at 3.01%/2.98% vs 3.05%/3.03%, June 2019 at 3.13%/no offer vs 3.17%/3.10%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades little changed Thursday at 25.700-25.750 from 25.71 late Wednesday, but up from 25.668 earlier. "It looks more like the koruna will trade in a range from 25.650 to 25.800, there doesn't seem to be much push for the koruna to stay at firmer levels below 25.700," Roman Fol, a Raiffeisenbank trader, says. The EUR/CZK is driven by the overall sentiment on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region. "There's some waiting on what'll happen elsewhere," Fol says. (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] The forint weakens against the euro as western European markets trade in the red, but HUF remains in the range seen for the past week, a trader says. EUR/HUF is at 281.10 versus 280.50 Wednesday afternoon, but the 278-282 range is likely to hold, Takarekbank says. USD/HUF is at 228.53 but the 225-230 range is unlikely to break, Takarekbank says. CHF/HUF is watched as CHF has been rallying since SNB signaled it would soften \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , negative news for \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large number of borrowers using CHF. CHF/HUF is at 207.58 and the next important levels are seen between 208-209, Takarekbank adds. CHF/HUF closed at 205.46 Wednesday. (margit.feher@dowjones.com) [ 06-24-10 0623ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100624e66o0007k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
