\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Hungarian Forint Weakens Within Range\b0\par\par\uc2 1,234 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2010\par 11:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] The forint weakens against the euro as western European markets trade in the red, but HUF remains in the range seen for the past week, a trader says. EUR/HUF is at 281.10 versus 280.50 Wednesday afternoon, but the 278-282 range is likely to hold, Takarekbank says. USD/HUF is at 228.53 but the 225-230 range is unlikely to break, Takarekbank says. CHF/HUF is watched as CHF has been rallying since SNB signaled it would soften \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , negative news for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's large number of borrowers using CHF. CHF/HUF is at 207.58 and the next important levels are seen between 208-209, Takarekbank adds. CHF/HUF closed at 205.46 Wednesday. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premia under pressure on view that spot gains may draw selling interest from firms looking to exit USD holdings now, says trader with foreign bank; 1-year forward premium at 1.32 vs 1.355 late Wednesday. Notes upward pressure on forwards in recent sessions stemming from concerns near-term interest rates may rise may also have been overdone, leaving forward premia vulnerable to correction. Still for today, major correction not on cards as spot move up largely driven by buying from large private engineering firm, capping of spot at current levels may ease downward pressure on premia.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] With sterling supported by the emergency budget and a hawk on the MPC, shorting EUR/GBP looks a good play for those with a negative euro view, says RBS. The cross has just made a fresh 19-month low of 0.8194. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] Data show Poland's retail sales were 3.5%-4% higher on the year in 1H and growth is expected to accelerate to 5%-6% on year in 2H, helped by the improving labor market, says Noble Bank economist Radoslaw Cholewinski. Cholewinski adds that the ongoing economic recovery could prove to be one of the reasons for a change, at end-3Q at the latest, of the informal monetary policy bias to tightening from neutral. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spread widens to 780 bps from an open at 770 bps in modest turnover of about EUR32M on the European and local HDAT platform. "There is continuous selling pressure from German, UK and French banks because Greek sovereigns have been dropped from several bond indexes following downgrades to junk from two major ratings agencies," says a senior local bond trader. "Because of low volumes magnifying spread movements we are likely to see levels before the IMF-EU bailout was put in place," he adds. (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares higher with oil stocks leading gains; benchmark KSE-100 index +0.9% at 9804.74. "Stocks registered gains following the visit by UK foreign secretary William Hauge to the Karachi Stock Exchange, assuring Pakistan that their government will offer around 665 million pounds over the next five years," says local dealer. Adnan Afridi, managing director of KSE, told reporters Hauge foresees deeper bilateral trade with Pakistan with enhanced investments from U.K. "The visit bodes well for the domestic capital markets and has aided sentiment today," says another dealer. Foreign investment in local equity market Wednesday amounted to $5.4 million. \uc2 Pakistan State Oil\uc2  (PSO.KA) +0.5% at PKR270.70, Attock Refinery (ATRL.KA) +0.5% at PKR298. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI ends down 0.3% at 1325.87 in moderate volume of 734 million shares. Local dealer says broader market mixed, index trading sideways in absence of leads; "we expect a similar performance tomorrow." Tips 1315-1335 range. Maybank Investment analyst Lee Cheng Hooi says trading opportunities have emerged following bullish engulfing candlestick low of 1243 in May. Thinks market may retest 1349, but advises investors to trade "cautiously;" sets stop-loss below 1308. Market breadth marginally negative with 332 decliners vs 317 advancers; Tanjong (2267.KU) down 2.0% at MYR17.74, HL Bank (5819.KU) down 2.6% at MYR8.55; \uc2 Berjaya Land\uc2  (4219.KU) +9.3% at MYR4.45, SP Setia (8664.KU) +2.5% at MYR4.10. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] The widening Greek/bund yield spreads continue to extend Thursday after Wednesday's big move as portfolio managers look to reduce their holdings of Greek bonds. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgraded Greek debt to Ba1 last week, which means it will not be included in the major bond indices to be reorganised at month end. Bond portfolio managers who use the indices as benchmarks have been selling Greek bond holdings ahead of this reallocation, says Cyril Beuzit, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  strategist. The 5-year Greek/bund yield spread trades at +948bps, versus Wednesday morning's +869bps, while the 10-year spread trades at +798bps versus +734bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher late on domestic fiscal concerns, tight USD supply while some investors turn risk averse as EUR weak in late Asian trade; pair last at 46.34 in heavy volume vs 45.935 intraday low, 46.05 late Wednesday; next resistance at 46.60, support at 46. "You have the budget deficit story, then a short squeeze," says local bank trader. Another trader says banks initially pushed USD/PHP down but when pair held above 45.90, market shifted gears, with weak regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fueling USD-buying. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish consumers see the ongoing economic recovery as permanent and are confident the labor market improvement will continue, says \uc2 Bank Millennium\uc2 . Adds Poland's unemployment will likely continue to decline in the coming months from 11.9% in May, but will rise again in the final months of this year to 12.0%. Says private consumption will fuel growth in 2Q and 2010. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is lower Thursday tracing the weaker euro, with investors ignoring the better-than-expected Polish May retail sales and unemployment figures, a trader says. Adds the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen rangebound at EUR/PLN 4.05-4.10. EUR/PLN is at 4.0878 from 4.0597 late Wednesday, USD/PLN is at 3.3327 from 3.2971. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.72% from 4.75%, 5-year at 5.44% from 5.45%, 10-year at 5.88% from 5.89%. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan average interbank overnight rate tad lower at 0.186% vs fresh 17-month high 0.187%; activity tepid as traders mostly stay on sidelines ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meeting later, says local bank trader. Tips rate biased up in near term; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may continue to guide short-term rate higher on large gap between overnight rate and rate of negotiable certificate of deposits," she says. Rate of 30-day NCDs/CDs at 0.57% recently. (mason.wu@dowjones.com) [ 06-24-10 0529ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100624e66o0006z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
