\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Forint gains could hurt economy, cbank may step in\b0\par\par\uc2 906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2010\par 12:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Forint seen extending gains after April 25 election\par \par \uc2 * New Fidesz govt, c.bank likely to oppose more forint gains\par \par \uc2 * Strong forint would hurt exports, engine of recovery\par \par \uc2 By Marton Dunai and Sandor Peto\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, April 21 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint is expected to rise further after Sunday's election, which could prompt the incoming pro-growth Fidesz government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains to help the economy.\par \par \uc2 An expected two-thirds majority for Fidesz, enough to launch deep reforms in the recession-hit economy, has beefed up market optimism, and the forint <EURHUF=> has outperformed its regional peers since the first round of voting on April 11, which Fidesz won by a landslide.\par \par \uc2 Since then the forint has firmed 1 percent to 263.70 versus the euro by 0630 GMT on Wednesday. The zloty <EURPLN=> of Poland, the only European economy to avoid recession, was flat while the Czech crown shed 0.3 percent in the same period.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say problems in the euro zone will not erode gains as investors have begun to differentiate between emerging Europe and Greece and the forint could rise further to levels which could hurt exports, the engine of recovery.\par \par \uc2 "At the current level it's not obvious a strong forint is in anyone's interest," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  analyst Pasquale Diana said.\par \par \uc2 "First, you don't need it from an inflationary standpoint," he said. "Second ... this economy is frankly humming on one cylinder (exports). This is not a time you need EUR/HUF heading south, especially if it is not warranted by fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 The forint's recent gains are inconsistent with levels seen in the previous 9 months, when it hardly deviated from the 270 per euro level. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also stronger than seen in the most recent Reuters forecasts. <FXEURHUF>\par \par \uc2 Before Fidesz's big victory in the first election round, most analysts predicted the forint would stay around 270 per euro in the next six months at least.\par \par \uc2 Now most of them see it rising further in the short term.\par \par \uc2 "In my view the forint will ... firm to between 250 and 260 in the short term," \uc2 Concorde Securities\uc2  analyst Janos Samu said. "Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will again see appreciation pressure."\par \par \uc2 Hungary's external financing capability, after deep budget cuts last year, is among the best in the region, also seen as a positive for the forint.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian bonds are also seen extending their strong rally that pushed yields to all-time lows. [ID:nLDE62U1OZ]\par \par \uc2 Credit default swap spreads have hit their lowest since before Hungary resorted to an IMF and EU bailout in 2008.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  TAKES NOTE\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also noted the forint's strength. Vice Governor Ferenc Karvalits told Reuters last week there was no need for tighter monetary conditions. [ID:nLDE63F0XW]\par \par \uc2 Karvalits said the Hungarian bank "generally refrains" from using \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but "does not categorically exclude" the use of this tool if needed.\par \par \uc2 He also said further cautious rate cuts from the current record low of 5.5 percent <NBHI> were on the cards.\par \par \uc2 Emboldened, markets may price deeper rate cuts than the bank is willing to deliver -- even after rate setters shifted toward a more dovish stance in March. [ID:nLDE63D1LR]\par \par \uc2 "We think that there is scope for front-end rates to overshoot on the downside, especially if the forint remains strong," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's Diana wrote in a recent note.\par \par \uc2 The NBH is fairly tolerant towards a strong forint, analysts said, but persistent pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could force rate cuts and possibly even direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 255 forints to the euro.\par \par \uc2 "The NBH can't fine-tune the exchange rate through interest rate cuts, so I'm inclined to think there will be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well," \uc2 MKB Bank\uc2  analyst Zsolt Kondrat said.\par \par \uc2 Poland and Romania have already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cool markets.\par \par \uc2 "(\b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is the exception, not the rule, as we have not deployed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet," Kondrat added.\par \par \uc2 Fidesz, whose top priority is economic growth, is likely to favour lower rates as well. That would boost corporate lending and weaken the forint to benefit exports, the sole driver of economic growth amid sluggish domestic consumption.\par \par \uc2 "For the economy, (EUR/HUF) 270 would be good," Samu said. "I think Fidesz will want an even weaker exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 An exceedingly strong forint may prompt the new Fidesz government to try to talk down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Balint Hada, an analyst at Quaestor Financial Consulting in Budapest.\par \par \uc2 But a positive scenario for Hungarian markets, including the forint, hinges on a healthy global background.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said risks could re-emerge in the second half of 2010. Liquidity in global markets may tighten and the new government may loosen the fiscal grip to restart growth.\par \par \uc2 "The current (global) optimism is fragile," Hada said. "My fear is that something might come, perhaps as early as this quarter, a bad economic figure which would shatter optimism. It would be much harder to navigate the (Hungarian) economy then."\par \par \uc2 A significant forint weakening towards 300 versus the euro would unnerve the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and put an end to monetary easing as it could threaten financial stability. According to a recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  study, it could also hurt the economy by forcing banks to tighten credit. (Editing by Stephen Nisbet)\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY-ELECTION/FORINT (ANALYSIS, PIX TV)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|HX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100424e64l0005r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
