\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Czech Aug Trade May Boost Koruna\b0\par\par\uc2 1,606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2009\par 05:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Go to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe}}}\uc2  for the new Dow Jones blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Marcin Sobczyk \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par 0900/0700 Hungarian September foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par 0900/0700 Czech August foreign trade \par 1200/1000 Czech finance ministry to auction around CZK7 billion of reopened 15-year bond issue \par 1200/1000 Polish two-year treasury bond tender \par 1400/1200 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sept. 28 meeting minutes \par  N/A      Supervisory board of Czech CSA Airlines to discuss cost-cutting measures and Unimex/Iceland Air bid for the indebted Czech flagship carrier \f28 \par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--The Czech Republic's foreign trade reading for August will set off this month's indicator season, which will climax Friday when the statistics office releases Czech September inflation data.\par \par \uc2 The August foreign trade balance, benefiting from contracting exports amid the recession, is expected show a surplus of about 8 billion koruna ($460 million), remaining in positive territory for the seventh month in a row.\par \par \uc2 The trade data may help the koruna rebound against the euro, following its losses Tuesday after comments by Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zdenek Tuma late Monday that the bank may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's firming.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will publish the minutes of its September rate meeting, when the overwhelming majority of the Monetary Policy Council members voted for a 50 basis-point cut in the policy rate to 7.50%, against a 75 basis-point rate cut, which was also discussed.\par \par \uc2 The MPC made dovish comments after the rate decision in September, opening the way for further rate cuts to an eventual key rate of around 6% by year-end, analysts have said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par        Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                   2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 25.6170-70 25.6360-60 -0.07  25.6620 25.6400  -4.25 \par EUR/HUF 266.52-93  266.65-06  -0.05  266.72  266.80   +1.13 \par EUR/PLN 4.1840-00  4.1855-75  -0.04  4.1870  4.1883   +1.68 \par USD/RUB 29.864-70  29.830-36  +0.11  29.871  29.813   -2.17 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the Hungarian forint, the euro/forint cross may go deeper and flirt with HUF265 Wednesday. The forint has strengthened against the euro following the upgrading of Hungary's credit rating outlook to neutral from negative. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been testing the HUF266.60 trendline support against the euro, which might prove problematic for exports, eventually, but this won't be the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's concern just yet, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Polish zloty is expected to trade within PLN4.15-PLN4.20 range against the euro, dealers said. "The zloty has strengthened recently after uncertainty disappeared regarding Eureko's dividend repatriation plans, and now the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stabilized within the range," a local dealer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna is unlikely to benefit from stock market gains as investors remain wary of a possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciation. The koruna is likely to trade around CZK25.53 against the euro, which is still stronger than the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's forecast of CZK25.70 for 2010. "Therefore the bank looks unfavorably at [the] koruna's recent gains," Raiffeisenbank analyst Michal Brozka said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the Russian ruble, a further collapse in the value of the U.S. dollar is likely to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "The ruble is likely to benefit as traders move into higher yielding developing economy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said UralSib.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FIXED INCOME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS - The average three-year benchmark yield was at 7.10% late Tuesday from 7.23% late Monday, five-year at 7.50% from 7.53%, and 10-year at 7.80% from 7.99%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian government bonds will likely tread water Wednesday after a calm trading day Tuesday, a trader said. He expected the bond yields to move only after Thursday's bond auction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BOND YIELDS - Polish bonds were mostly stronger Tuesday in thin trade. The average yield on the two-year paper was at 5.10% late Tuesday from 5.14% late Monday, the five-year at 5.71% from 5.73%, 10-year is flat at 6.20%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, bonds' performance will depend on the results of two-year paper auction as the finance ministry offers up to PLN4.5 billion worth of paper. Otherwise, the debt market will follow the zloty's moves, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BOND YIELD - The yield on the benchmark 10-year paper was at 4.80% late Tuesday, compared with 4.77% Monday afternoon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The auction of the reopened 15-year Czech koruna bond, carrying a 5.70% coupon, will be center stage on the local stock market. Some traders have said that the auction may serve as a litmus test to how seriously investors take the local political stalemate and concerns about the widening budget gap in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \par  \par WIG20                   2222.27  gain   71.68  up 3.3% \par BUX                    20286.04  gain  501.56  up 2.5% \par PX                      1144.20  gain   20.60  up 1.8% \par RTS                     1270.67  gain   43.48  up 3.5% \par Stoxx EU Enlarged 15   2155.45   gain   64.89  up 3.1% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: After the WIG20 index ended at 2222.27 in heavy trade, current rises on U.S. equities look likely to give Warsaw's large-cap index a boost Wednesday as well, a dealer said. Tuesday's rally was fueled by hopeful global sentiment about the coming U.S. earnings season, the dealer added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Prague blue-chip stocks may face some profit-taking Wednesday, following gains for two straight days. "Selloffs can't be ruled out, but they can be limited or completely averted if core markets in Europe remain strong," a local trader said, adding that any gains in the U.S. may also help Czech stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: Hungarian stocks MOL and OTP are likely in the coming days to break the weakening trend of the past few sessions, a broker said. The two blue-chip stocks are expected to test resistance levels of HUF16,000 and HUF5,500, respectively. MOL ended up 4.7% Tuesday at HUF15,280 and OTP closed 4.6% higher at HUF5,360.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: The mood on the Russian stock market should remain positive Wednesday, a Moscow-based trader said. "This recovery has been too fast, but funds still have cash to invest," the trader said. [ 10-06-09 2311ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: No Russian scientists are involved in the development of the Iranian nuclear program, Russian National Security Consoler Nikolai Patrushev said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: For the first time in more than a decade, Russia will need to tap the international capital market for some much needed capital, and investors can't wait. Russia will start a preliminary roadshow for Eurobond issues early next month in London and will launch a sovereign bond in February or later, the Interfax news agency reported Tuesday, citing Deputy Finance Minister Dmitry Pankin.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Ukraine has paid Russia for natural gas it consumed in September, Ukrainian energy company Naftogaz said Tuesday. "We paid for the gas in full and on time," said Naftogaz spokesman Valentyn Zemlyansky, according to the RIA Novosti news agency.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: In the first eight months of 2009 trade between Russia and Belarus fell to the 2005 level, said Belarusian Prime Minister Sergei Sidorsky. Trade fell by over 40%, though fluctuations in Russia's and Belarus' gross domestic products are not so significant compared to the slump in bilateral trade, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russia's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has bought a 25% stake in the Russian Auction House, the bank said in a report obtained by PRIME-TASS on Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's treasury said Tuesday it had accepted six preliminary offers from brokerages and banks for minority stakes in the state-owned Warsaw Stock Exchange, which is in the process of being privatized.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's treasury said Tuesday it had accepted six preliminary offers from brokerages and banks for minority stakes in the state-owned Warsaw Stock Exchange, which is in the process of being privatized.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's troubled flag-carrying airline LOT said Tuesday it planned by next year to cut 400 jobs from its current 3,500-strong payroll under a deal between management and unions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy board will continue to actively debate employing policy tools other than interest rates to ease monetary conditions further, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Vice Governor Miroslav Singer said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: US Vice President Joe Biden will visit the Czech Republic and Poland later this month following a political storm over the US decision to shelve plans to host a missile shield in the two countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romania's budget deficit was 5% of gross domestic product after the first nine months of 2009, less than the 5.4% limit agreed with the International Monetary Fund, Finance Minister Gheorghe Pogea said Tuesday, news agency Mediafax reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: AES Geo Energy, a Bulgarian unit of US energy giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 AES Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , launched on Tuesday the largest 156-megawatt wind farm in Bulgaria, the company said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Marcin Sobczyk, Dow Jones Newswires; +4822 447-2432; marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jacob Pedersen in Moscow, Marynia Kruk and Malgorzata Halaba in Warsaw, Leos Rousek in Prague, and Margit Feher and Veronika Gulyas in Budapest contributed to this article.) [ 10-06-09 2311ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091007e5a70000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
