\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 4-Forint weaker after rate cut; leu rebounds\b0\par\par\uc2 950 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2009\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Hungary cuts rates by 50 bps as expected\par \par \uc2  * Romanian leu rebounds, dealers cite c.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  * Zloty rebounds, fears over dividend payout fade\par \par \uc2     (Updates throughout)\par \par \uc2  By Marius Zaharia and Dagmara Leszkowicz\par \par \uc2  BUCHAREST/WARSAW, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint was down slightly after an interest rate cut on Monday, while the Romanian leu regained morning losses with dealers saying covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was behind the move.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates by 50 basis points, bringing the key rate to 7.5 percent. [ID:nLS226531]\par \par \uc2  "In case market conditions remain supportive and Hungary's risk assessment does not deteriorate, further cuts will be possible this year, taking the rate down to 7 percent or perhaps even below that level," said Gyorgy Barta at CIB Bank.\par \par \uc2  At 1318 GMT the forint <EURHUF=> was 0.2 percent weaker to the euro, trading at 269.43, while the country's short-end paper yields fell slightly and long-dated bonds rose after the decision.\par \par \uc2  Romania's leu <EURRON=> was 0.1 percent down to the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily. They probably sold over 200 million euros today," one dealer said.\par \par \uc2  Dealers speculate this adds to around 300-400 million euros it may have sold last week and to about 4.5 billion euros analysts estimate it had sold since the \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  collapse to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  countered weakening pressure stemming from coalition rifts.\par \par \uc2  Political risk came back in focus in Romania after the government survived a no-confidence vote last week and the centrist prime minister sacked a leftist minister on Monday , increasing tensions in the ruling coalition ahead of presidential polls. [ID:nLS228602]\par \par \uc2  Romania is  seen cutting rates this week [ID:nL8336631] while Poland is expected to hold fire. [ID:nLB85085]\par \par \uc2  In Poland, the zloty <EURPLN=> was almost flat, reversing some of its previous losses after it had hit a fresh two-week low following stock losses globally.\par \par \uc2  "It looks like big London names decided to buy the zloty again as a fear over a possible (PZU) dividend payout somehow subsided," said one Warsaw-based dealer.\par \par \uc2  Last week Poland agreed with Dutch firm Eureko [EUREK.UL] to postpone for a week a shareholders meeting of Polish insurer PZU that may decide on a big dividend payout aimed at helping settle a long-running dispute between the two owners [ID:nLO277474].\par \par \uc2  Polish bonds also reversed previous losses, tracking the zloty.\par \par \uc2  BUDGET WOES\par \par \uc2  Poland's public debt is expected to rise to 54.7 percent of GDP next year, close to the constitutional ceiling of 55 percent which will force it to significantly cut spending and present a balanced budget, a news agency reported [ID:nLS216692].\par \par \uc2  In Czech Republic, the crown <EURCZK=> edged down as domestic markets were closed for a holiday. On Friday, investors were relieved to see the parliament approving a 2010 austerity plan, removing the risk of a government collapse [ID:nLP440303].\par \par \uc2  Markets were awaiting comments from policymakers, bankers and corporate officials on the strength of a recovery in the region at the Reuters Central and Eastern European investment Summit from Sept. 28-30 [ID:nLN293273].\par \par \uc2  The region's second-largest lender, Raiffeisen International  said non-performing loans will peak next year, and that the bank started to ease credit conditions again in some countries as \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have stabilised [ID:nLAG003779]. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                                            today    in 2009 \par Czech crown      <EURCZK=>  25.207   25.148  -0.23%   +6.13%\par Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>   4.201    4.2    -0.02%   -2.05%\par Hungarian forint <EURHUF=> 269.43   268.91   -0.19%   -2.18%\par Croatian kuna    <EURHRK=>   7.28     7.267  -0.18%   +1.17%\par Romanian leu     <EURRON=>   4.2      4.196  -0.1%    -4.42%\par Serbian dinar    <EURRSD=>  92.43    92.71   +0.3%    -3.19%\par Yield Spreads\par Czech treasury bonds <0#CZBMK=>\par 3-yr T-bond   CZ3YT=RR    -4 basis points to  167bps over bmk*\par 7-yr T-bond   CZ7YT=RR    -1 basis points to  +183bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond   CZ10YT=RR    -13 basis points to  +176bps over\par bmk*\par Polish treasury bonds <0#PLBMK=>\par 2-yr T-bond   PL2YT=RR   +1 basis points to  +388bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond   PL5YT=RR    -1 basis points to  +332bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond PL10YT=RR    -2 basis points to  +292bps over bmk*\par Hungarian treasury bond