\par\par \b \uc2 Hungary MPC:Bking System Faces Challenges, Has Ample Buffers\b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2009\par 14:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Edited Press Release \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--Hungary's banking sector is facing challenges as a result of the decline in gross domestic product and investor confidence but it has sufficient capital buffers to absorb possible losses, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Monetary policy Council said Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The negative effects of the global economic crisis have increased significantly and both the private and banking sectors have deleveraged their balance sheets in the past six months. As financing conditions deteriorated, global economic activity weakened more sharply than expected, and liquidity problems in the global financial system became more acute.\par \par \uc2 Hungary couldn't remain unaffected by these adverse influences due to the high degree of its economy's openness and deep financial and trade integration.\par \par \uc2 The increase in indebtedness of the country and the public sector, the large amount of the private sector foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated debt and the substantial weakening in the economy's growth potential added to negative market sentiment toward Hungary.\par \par \uc2 In Hungary, as in other countries, firms have adjusted to the adverse economic and financial market environment by reducing their demand for credit and curtailing their activities. Banks have increasingly sought to attract household deposits, in order to reduce their dependence on money markets, and tightened the supply of credit in response to the decline in risk tolerance.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting these developments, Hungary's net external financing requirement is expected to fall, but the economy is likely to slip into recession.\par \par \uc2 If firms' and banks' funding fell excessively in response to the worse-than-expected external and domestic conditions, it would lead to a deeper and more protracted economic downturn. That, in turn, would increase risks to financial stability.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian banks have been exposed to increasing liquidity and capital adequacy risks due to deteriorating financial market conditions and the unfavorable economic environment.\par \par \uc2 However, liquidity risks may be mitigated by the strong commitment of foreign parent banks to their Hungarian subsidiaries, the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2 's actions to enhance forint and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity, and the Hungarian government's liquidity support facility, which ensures that credit institutions have easier access to funds.\par \par \uc2 The economic downturn, the rise in the costs of external funding and the weakening of the exchange rate have had adverse effects on domestic banks' solvency through a slowdown in lending and a deterioration in loan portfolio quality.\par \par \uc2 However, low Hungarian loan-to-value ratios by international standards, the absence of a house price bubble, households' strong propensity to repay their debts and banks' flexibility in clients' debt management can help mitigate losses arising from increasing risks.\par \par \uc2 Currently, the banking sector has sufficient capital buffers to absorb losses.\par \par \uc2 However, strengthening banks' capital position further could help counteract the negative effects of the worse-than-expected economic and financial market environment.\par \par \uc2 The authorities responsible for maintaining financial stability should respond to changes in the operating environment of the financial system by broadening the range of their instruments and improving risk-based oversight.\par \par \uc2 In addition to individual institutions, problems affecting the entire financial system should be monitored on a continuous basis, and there needs to be a shift in focus from ex-post \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevention.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this goal may be achieved by taking regulatory actions to expand the powers of the supervisory authority, managing the risks of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending, strengthening lending discipline - with special regard to lending through agents-, reducing the procyclical behavior of the financial system, limiting maturity mismatches, and developing banks' credit and liquidity risk management.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  will take actions and make proposals to ensure that the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , oversight and regulation of the financial system can meet these new challenges as soon as possible.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Web site: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mnb.hu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.mnb.hu}}}\uc2  [ 06-04-09 1241GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090406e546000ho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
