\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Polish Core CPI, Ctrl Bk Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 By David McQuaid   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 04:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par N/A/N/A   National Bank Of Hungary hosts CEE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors \par 1400/1300 Poland Feb Core Inflation, Seen +2.6% On Year Vs Jan +2.6% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--Polish core inflation data for January and February head off a thin events schedule Friday in Central and Eastern Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key core inflation indicator, which strips food and energy prices from the consumer price index, is seen holding steady at 2.6% on the year in both January and February, a slight slowdown from December's 2.8% reading.\par \par \uc2 Eyes will also be on Budapest, where the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  hosts \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from Austria, the Czech Republic, Poland, Slovenia and Slovakia at a meeting closed to the media.\par \par \uc2 Mid-February's record-low levels of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still very much in mind, but analysts aren't expecting any concerted action or even verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the zloty, the koruna, and the forint now trading stronger.\par \par \uc2 "Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be superfluous now," said a Budapest-based trader.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 26.834-04  26.857-87  -0.09  26.854  26.884   +0.30 \par EUR/HUF 301.53-82  302.15-45  -0.21  301.72  301.28  +14.41 \par EUR/PLN 4.6244-04  4.6219-59  +0.05  Untraded today  +12.38 \par USD/RUB 33.342-48  33.348-54  -0.02  33.405  33.331   +9.23 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forint can't seem to sustain gains below 300 against the euro, popping above that psychological level late Thursday after having firmed considerably on the soaring euro/dollar cross.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday's Hungarian rate decision won't provide much relief. True, analysts agree concern about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from cutting rates despite a gruesome economic outlook. But a rate hike is seen as a measure of last resort to be used only in case of another violent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  selloff, says Medley Global Advisors in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The zloty is also feeling the strain despite the stronger euro/dollar. However, strong technical support at 4.60 against the euro is holding, suggesting there is near-term upside to 4.55 or even higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The koruna is seen hovering around 26.70 against the euro, but could test resistance at 26.60 or even 26.50 if global news flow remains positive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ruble's up-trend could also continue if surging oil prices provide the fuel. Thursday, traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from rising too quickly. It ended the day at RUB38.86 against the dollar-euro basket, from RUB39.26 earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS - The three-year bond yield was at 12.25% late Thursday, from 12.45% late Wednesday; five-year was at 12.40% from 12.10%; 10-year was at 12% from 11.55%. \par POLISH BOND YIELDS - The two-year average yield was at 5.72% late Thursday from 5.75% late Wednesday; five-year unchanged at 6.06%; 10-year was at 6.29% from 6.30%. \par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD - 10-year benchmark was at 5.08% late Thursday, unchanged from late Wednesday. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's bond market is developing a pronounced split personality, as government buybacks prop up short yields, while long maturity bonds are in full retreat despite positive global sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish bonds are little moved after the publication of last month's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  minutes, which confirmed the Monetary Policy Council was mainly troubled about the weak zloty and so chose a small-steps approach of easing by 25 basis-point increments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                    1487.71   gain   34.9   up 2.4% \par BUX                     10254.36   gain  357.48  up 3.6% \par PX                        706.3    gain   36.3   up 5.1% \par RTS                       694.69   gain   44.07  up 6.8% \par DJ Stoxx EU Enlarged 15: 1275.10   gain   36.28  up 2.9% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: Friday's session could see much higher volatility among blue chips as March WIG20 index futures expire, a local analyst says. The weaker dollar drove gains by commodities issues Thursday, especially KGHM, which jumped 5.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Profit-taking could be in order Friday after another hefty advance, especially by Erste, which soared 19% on improved investor sentiment toward regional banks. "The stock's been so heavily oversold that it keeps firming," says one trader.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: The prospects for further gains appear uncertain, with U.S. stocks starting to correct, traders say. However, one advancer that could have staying power is Thursday's star, OTP, which ended the day up 7.7%. "What we've seen today (from OTP) is possibly the start of a long-term rebound," a trader says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW:Traders are starting to get queasy about this week's breakneck rally, which saw the RTS set a new 2009 high Thursday. "Strong commodities are good for emerging markets but it's not been as busy as we thought - buyers can't stomach the surge in prices and sellers are holding out for higher levels," said one broker, who isn't certain the gains from $50 a barrel crude can be sustained. [ 03-19-09 2355ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Some of Russia's strategic companies may go bankrupt if the government denies them support, Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin told a briefing after a government meeting on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russia's anti-crisis program for 2009, circulated by the government's press service on Thursday, stipulates tighter measures against bribe-extorting interference in businesses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russian President Dmitry Medvedev met with his Belarusian counterpart Alexander Lukashenko on Thursday to discuss anti-crisis measures amid the ongoing global economic downturn, the president's press secretary said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: The Russian government will sell its\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par industrial and financial assets during post-crisis recovery, according to Interfax. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Net capital outflow from Russia is expected to amount to $80 billion in 2009, Economic Development Minister Elvira Nabiullina told reporters Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: State-owned railroad monopoly Russian Railways is expected to complete offering seven infrastructure bond issues worth a total of 100 billion rubles by the end of May, according to an economic stimulus plan drafted by the government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland and France were at odds Thursday over a European Union Commission plan to grant EUR600 million in aid to six ex-Soviet states, with Paris fearing it would lessen the pot for Mediterranean nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk expressed hope Thursday the European Union will earmark funds for use in the Nabucco gas pipeline, a key project for bringing Caspian energy to Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: The supervisory board of Poland's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Handlowy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BHW.WA), majority owned by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (C), recommended Thursday that the bank doesn't pay a dividend from its 2008 profit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EU: European Commission President Jose Manuel Barroso Thursday said he expects members of the European Union to approve a doubling of the bloc's funds for helping struggling economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is thought to have been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in support of the forint through the Bank for International Settlements, Hungarian business weekly Figyelo said Thursday, citing unnamed sources.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romanian President Traian Basescu said Thursday the European Investment Bank, or EIB, will approve a EUR400 million loan for Ford by the end of April, while a EUR200 million loan for Renault is expected to be approved in the second half of 2009, news agency Mediafax reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By David McQuaid, Dow Jones Newswires; +48 22 528- 6776; david.mcquaid@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Andrew Langley in Moscow, Edith Balazs in Budapest, Marek Strzelecki in Warsaw, and Leos Rousek in Prague contributed to this report.) [ 03-19-09 2355ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020090320e53k0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict