\par\par \b \uc2 Hungary's forint falls 2 pct vs euro\b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 12:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, March 12 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint <EURHUF=D2> fell 2 percent versus the euro on Thursday, leading \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses in central and eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 A speculative reversal of positions by some big foreign players contributed to the forint's latest decline after a rebound earlier this week on measures taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The forint fell to 308.10 versus the euro from its opening level of 302, before recovering slightly to 307 by 1130 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "The forint again seems to be decoupling (from the region)," a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said.\par \par \uc2 "The bank's likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the forint firmer (in the past three days), the backdrop has not changed, so we were bound to weaken again," another dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week the forint surged from record lows at 317.45 last week, after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) Governor Andras Simor hinted at market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Reporting by Krisztina Than, editing by Mike Peacock)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HUNGARY\b0 \highlight\uc2 -FORINT/ (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|HX|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090312e53c000y0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
