\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-CEE picture darkens as exports, consumption fall\b0\par\par\uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 13:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Q4 GDP revised down across central and eastern Europe\par \par \uc2 * Baltic economies contract by around 10 pct y/y\par \par \uc2 * Data point to deeper downturn this year\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise on global sentiment, c.bank support\par \par \uc2 By Jan Lopatka\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, March 11 (Reuters) - Central and eastern European economies have entered a more severe downturn than was thought just weeks ago as their key export markets in the west collapse and domestic demand plunges, data showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Statisticians in the Czech Republic, Hungary, Bulgaria and Estonia all slashed their original estimates of economic output in the fourth quarter, showing recession was on the way in most of a region now badly hit by the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Latvia revised its GDP up marginally but still reported a 10.3 percent slump, a downturn that has led to a government collapse and violent protests in the small Baltic country. [ID:nRIG000042]\par \par \uc2 The data varied from Latvia's double-digit contraction to 3.5 percent growth in Bulgaria, showing a vast gap within the region, but all were headed for a poorer-than-expected 2009.\par \par \uc2 The region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended a rally despite the bleak data, with the Czech crown breaking even year-to-date, as markets drew support from globally better sentiment, absorbed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  and banked on a possible bailout package for Romania. [ID:nLB7011394]\par \par \uc2 "There is nothing yet that would signify a turnaround in this region, or abroad in fact. It is still too early to say where the bottom is; we are not there yet," said Agata Urbanska, an analyst at ING in London.\par \par \uc2 "Increasingly it is not differentiation between one economy growing and another contracting; it's just differentiation between how much economies are going to contract."\par \par \uc2 Poland remained optimistic on its performance this year, with Finance Minister Jacek Rostowski predicting 1.7 to 3.7 percent growth, above analysts' estimates of 1.2 percent. [ID:nWAR008034]\par \par \uc2 The Czech economy shrunk by 0.9 percent in the final quarter, compared with the previous three months, the worst figure since 1997 and a deterioration from the originally reported 0.6 percent drop. It eked out 0.7 percent year-on-year growth. [ID:nLB447882]\par \par \uc2 EURO HURT\par \par \uc2 The main culprit was exports, the key growth driver in past years which evaporated as demand in Germany and other west European markets plunged. Exports fell 24 percent year-on-year in January, showing the economy continued to shrink.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said Czech output would suffer badly but the country remained relatively well positioned to ride out the recession without a meltdown thanks to low current account and budget gaps -- which allows it to ease fiscal policy -- and small exposure to the foreign debt that has crippled others.\par \par \uc2 Hungary, in contrast badly hit by a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drop that has inflated its banks' and households' mountain of foreign debt, recorded a 1.2 percent quarterly drop, the third decline in a row that put the annual decline at 2.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The main cause, as in the Czech Republic, was weak exports strangling the manufacturing sector. Analysts see the economy shrinking by 3.6 percent this year. <HUGDP1>\par \par \uc2 Hungary has been eating through a $25 billion rescue loan provided by the International Monetary Fund and the European Union as it struggles to start selling its own debt again.\par \par \uc2 "The data point to a deeper recession setting in quicker than expected," said Michal Dybula of \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Moving forward we expect the contraction in year-on-year terms to deepen, driven by a combination of falling external and domestic demand, the latter undermined by a tight policy mix."\par \par \uc2 TOTAL GLOOM IN BALTICS\par \par \uc2 Estonia, another small Baltic country badly hit by a halt in credit flows, reported a 9.7 percent slump in fourth-quarter gross domestic product year-on-year, led by a collapse of household demand and investments.\par \par \uc2 Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was leaning towards a bleak scenario forecasting an 8.9 percent slump for all of 2009. [ID:nLB202332] Bulgaria revised its fourth-quarter year-on-year growth marginally to 3.5 percent from 3.6 percent, versus 6.8 percent the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Romania reported an uptick in inflation to 6.9 percent in February, driven by a weaker leu \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and undermining the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's options to cut interest rates to support the economy. [ID:nLB580883]\par \par \uc2 (For more CEE coverage, click on <TOP/EAST>)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL-EASTEUROPE/ (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|CZS|PX|HX|DA|FN|NW|SW|RDN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|BNX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090311e53b000sk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
