\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  defy bleak data as mkt wary of  \highlight22\uc2 Hungary \highlight\uc2  cbank\b0\par\par\uc2 1,064 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Regional growth outlook darkens\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up on Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumour\par \par \uc2 * Recent rally a short correction rather than trend reversal\par \par \uc2 * Markets await more news on potential Romanian aid package\par \par \uc2 * Hungary, Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shrug off GDP data\par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Dagmara Leszkowicz and Marius Zaharia\par \par \uc2 WARSAW/BUCHAREST, March 11 (Reuters) - Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  defied a darkening regional outlook on Wednesday, with Poland's zloty and Hungary's forint leading gains and helping the Czech crown almost erase this year's losses.\par \par \uc2 Fresh economic data showed the region, now severely hurt by the global financial crisis, entered a sharper downturn than was thought just weeks ago, with recession knocking on most doors [ID:nLB54784].\par \par \uc2 But market players stayed wary that Hungarian policymakers could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the battered forint after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Andras Simor indicated on Tuesday the bank may have sold euros from its reserves or shifted government EU funds through the market.\par \par \uc2 Regional markets also drew support from a globally better sentiment, with stocks rising on bank hopes [ID:nLB647450].\par \par \uc2 Romania's leu <EURRON=> was mostly stable, and dealers said markets were awaiting more details on a potential IMF-led bailout package a day after the government asked for international aid to avoid a financing crisis.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the forint leapt about 1.8 percent, pulling the Polish zloty and the Czech crown higher. The crown <EURCZK=> briefly clawed back its losses this year to break even at 26.69 per euro before sliding back to around 26.96 by 1413 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "There is a bit of speculation that the Hungarians are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  but I don't think that is the case. People are talking about it ... but the locals don't seem to think so," a London-based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 The forint <EURHUF=> stabilised towards the end of the domestic session, trading at 299.4 per euro.\par \par \uc2 "We are at a psychological level at 300 forints to the euro," one dealer said. "Hedge funds may want to see it weaker, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants it stronger, and we're moving sideways."\par \par \uc2 "Unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , significant moves are unlikely," he added.\par \par \uc2 Analysts warn this week's rally is likely to be short-lived, as the region has not yet seen the worse of the financial crisis and plunging economic activity as well as mounting worries about external financing will create more damage.\par \par \uc2 Czech and Hungarian growth data for the fourth quarter were worse than preliminary estimates. Czech growth was revised down to 0.7 percent on an annual basis, while Hungary contracted 2.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Poland's finance minister was more bullish, saying growth would be between 1.7 and 3.7 percent in 2009, a more optimistic outlook than the market consensus [ID:nWAR008034].\par \par \uc2 Although Poland's forecasts shape up better than its peers', the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to cut rates to support growth, its governor said [ID:nWAR008037].\par \par \uc2 The unit <EURPLN=> -- seen by analysts as a proxy for central and eastern Europe as Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is larger than its peers -- gained 1.8 percent to 4.589 per euro.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the zloty was likely to follow global markets for the time being, but has lost almost about a third of its value since hitting record highs last summer.\par \par \uc2 The reversal has hit companies with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options and forward or swap contracts, and the financial regulator said losses totalled $5 million as of mid-February. [ID:nWAR008041]\par \par \uc2 Romania and Hungary released February inflation data above expectations, strengthening expectations for interest rates to remain flat in Romania and be hiked in its western neighbour.\par \par \uc2 Dealers have said the leu, which has been relatively stable compared to its peers over the past weeks, could be moved near-term only be the announcement of the amount Romania plans to get from the EU, IMF and other financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 Bond markets also benefited from improving global sentiment, despite worse than expected GDP data, while in Hungary, talk of a reversal of the rate easing cycle did not impress investors.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy has its limits," one trader said. "A rate hike is not some magic weapon." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT-------------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                                            today    in 2009 \par Czech crown      <EURCZK=>  26.96    27.098  +0.51%    -0.77%\par Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>   4.589    4.671  +1.79%   -10.33%\par Hungarian forint <E