\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3-Romania next EU state to join bailout list\b0\par\par\uc2 938 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Romania asks EU executive for aid to avoid crisis\par \par \uc2 * European Commission says ready to provide support\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cbank says it may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , boosts mkts\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says emerging European states not all the same (Adds European Commission statement in paragraphs 3-4)\par \par \uc2 By Radu Marinas\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST, March 10 (Reuters) - Romania asked the EU's executive for aid on Tuesday to save it from a possible financing crisis, making it the latest member of the 27-member bloc to cry for outside help against the global economic storm.\par \par \uc2 Its plea coincided with a signal by Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it may have sold euros in the open market, boosting the forint and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have been battered since the global crisis hit emerging Europe last year.\par \par \uc2 Brussels was quick to reply, saying it is ready to offer aid.\par \par \uc2 "The EU Presidency and the Commission express ... readiness to support Romania in avoiding risks of increasing market pressures and in achieving the orderly unwinding of imbalances," the European Commission said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 All of central and Eastern Europe is scrambling to stop an evaporation of foreign funds that has caused Hungary and Latvia to grab International Monetary Fund-led lifelines, raised the risk of lending default, and sparked some public unrest.\par \par \uc2 Romania's finance ministry said it and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had started talks with the Commission, the IMF and other institutions to seek "medium-term foreign financial assistance," code-language for a financial bailout.\par \par \uc2 It followed a call on Monday by President Traian Basescu for popular support for belt-tightening measures and an IMF loan.\par \par \uc2 An EU source familiar with the talks said an agreement could be reached very quickly.\par \par \uc2 "Negotiations will start very soon. When they are concluded will depend on conditions to be proposed for the aid package and whether the Romanian government accepts right away," the source said.\par \par \uc2 An IMF mission will arrive in Bucharest on Wednesday for two weeks. Mihai Tanasescu, Bucharest's representative to the Fund, said he saw a potential programme running at least two years.\par \par \uc2 The European Commission has pledged to help struggling economies in the bloc's eastern wing on an ad hoc basis. But lacking broad support, it has baulked at a wider plan to cover the region as a whole and has so far let the International Monetary Fund lead bailout packages. Such deals require austerity steps that are all the more unpopular because they cut state spending in countries already facing a collapse in growth.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, EU finance ministers backed a call from the IMF to double its crisis funds to $500 billion, with an emphasis that states like China and Saudi Arabia could pay a big share.\par \par \uc2 BELT TIGHTENING\par \par \uc2 There have been no details on a potential bailout, but economists say it could amount to around 20 billion euros, near the $25 billion of IMF, EU and World Bank money agreed in a bailout of Hungary last October.\par \par \uc2 Romania's centre-left government, however, may yet struggle to meet the IMF's requirements, bound to include tough spending cuts for the country of 22 million.\par \par \uc2 "A smooth process should not be taken for granted," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said in a report. "The mettle of the coalition government is likely to be tested by the difficult decisions ahead."\par \par \uc2 The leu, which has lost 18 percent since last summer but has been stable in recent weeks, edged up but still trailed gains of as much as 1.9 percent in the forint, the Czech crown and Polish zloty following the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement.\par \par \uc2 "We are also active in the market, which means we buy forints against euros, but as to when and how much, as to how much we did yesterday and whether we did anything at all or what we are doing today -- we have not commented on that and will not comment on it in the future either," Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Andras Simor told TV2 television.\par \par \uc2 The bank said after a special meeting on Sunday it would start to channel EU funds into the market and was ready to use its entire tool kit to help the forint, but it was not clear if Simor was referring to EU funds or an actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ratings agency \uc2 Moody's\uc2  also said emerging European states should not be treated as if pressures on their credit-worthiness were uniform. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  sparked a region-wide sell-off last month after warning its banks could face ratings downgrades.\par \par \uc2 TENSION GROWS\par \par \uc2 After several years of booming growth, the 10 ex-communist states that have joined the EU since 2004 have been walloped by the economic crisis due to the dearth of foreign financing as well as a collapse in western demand that has hammered exports.\par \par \uc2 The assets sell-off has cut up to a third off the value of floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Polish zloty and put governments with units pegged to the euro under pressure to devalue, a move that would hit millions who took loans in euros and Swiss francs in the belief they would soon join the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Social unrest, including violent protests in Bulgaria and Latvia, has risen in some countries, and the latter country's government was forced to step down last month. (Additional reporting by Nerijus Adomaitis and Krisztina Than; Writing by Michael Winfrey; Editing by Kenneth Barry)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL-EASTEUROPE/IMF (WRAPUP 3)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|G|RBN|BNX|ABX|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090310e53a000xr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
