\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Converting Hungary EU funding on  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  market not viable\b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2009\par 10:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with detail, background)\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Feb 25 (Reuters) - Converting Hungary's funds from the European Union on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets rather than at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a viable option to support its ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Janos Veres said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Hungary expected around 400 million euros worth of funding from the EU in the remainder of this year and exchanging such a small amount would do little to support the forint in the medium to long term, Veres told a conference.\par \par \uc2 Veres' comments come after the Polish finance ministry sold some EU funds on the open market last week to boost the zloty. "We looked at the possibility of converting EU transfers on the market and not at the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2 ," Veres said. "But we're only talking about 400 million euros, this isn't an amount which could fundamentally alter the exchange rate, especially not in the medium to long term," Veres said.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's forint hit an all-time-low of 310 versus the euro earlier this month on mounting concerns about the economy, which is expected to shrink by 3.5 percent this year, the stability of the region's banking sector and general flight from emerging market assets.\par \par \uc2 But Veres said the government remained concerned over the volatility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "These exchange rate movements which happened recently are not favourable for the Hungarian economy," Veres said.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Poland, the Czech Republic and Romania \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally in a coordinated, joint move on Monday, which helped \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover slightly.\par \par \uc2 At 0927 GMT, the forint traded at 297.89 versus the euro, stronger from an opening level around 300. (Reporting by Balazs Koranyi; editing by Chris Pizzey)\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY-FORINT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|HX|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090225e52p000lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
