\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING:  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  May Stabilize\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2009\par 05:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par N/A       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  4Q 2008 earnings (expected early morning) \par N/A       Hungarian PM Gyurcsany meets EC President Barroso regarding \par           Hungary's measures to tackle the economic crisis \par 0800/0700 Miner New World Resources 2008 Earnings, followed by conference \par           call at 1000 GMT \par 1000/0900 Poland Retail Sales in Jan; seen +0.6%YY vs +6.6%YY \par 1000/0900 Poland Unemployment in Jan; seen 10.5% vs 9.5% \par 1130/1030 Hungary 3-Mo T-Bill tender \par 1200/1100 Hungary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  2-Week Bill tender \par 1630/1530 Czech Telefonica O2 4Q 2008 Earnings \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PRAGUE (Dow Jones)--Regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are expected to stabilize Tuesday after gaining Monday on comments by four of the region's central bankers that recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness has been overdone.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of Poland, Hungary, Romania and the Czech Republic all spoke out against the recent selloffs of their national \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened roughly 2% each on the day following the comments.\par \par \uc2 "The statements from the governors were released separately but were clearly coordinated and all more or less said the same thing: The recent selloff in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region has been overdone and does not reflect fundamentals," said Lars Christensen, chief analyst at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't enough to prevent further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening, but the growing coordination among the central bankers is reassuring to the markets, Christensen added.\par \par \uc2 Separately Tuesday, Hungarian Prime Minister Ferenc Gyurcsany will meet European Commission President Jose Manuel Barroso to inform him of the Hungarian government's measures to tackle the impact of the economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Also Tuesday, Polish retail data should show consumers closing their wallets as the economy weakens, and several regional companies report 2008 earnings.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 28.300-40  28.403-53  -0.36  28.393  28.335   +5.78 \par EUR/HUF 297.48-78  297.60-90  -0.04  298.70  297.52  +12.87 \par EUR/PLN 4.6660-10  4.6390-40  +0.58  4.6691  4.6540  +13.39 \par USD/RUB 36.075-07  36.111-43  -0.10  36.175  36.036  +18.18 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the concerted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comments Monday, the market is now looking for action to replace words, either via regional central banks hiking rates or by their getting outside help, potentially from the ECB.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  said comments alone are most likely not sufficient to maintain improved regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels, and Danske said they are looking for the ECB to shed light on whether it will step into the markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet some traders think that markets may have gotten the message. With the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increasing the pressure on markets to stop the selloffs, the zloty looks set to trade to the euro well below the 5.0 level. "The time of euro/zloty at 4.90 are behind us for now. The zloty should stay flat in the next few days," a dealer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Prague, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Zdenek Tuma said the koruna's 14% weakening against the euro since early October can't be explained by fundamental factors. Euro/koruna moved down about 1.8% on the day after Tuma's comment to end at 28.32.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "(CEE central bankers) share the same opinion that the recent exchange rate fluctuation in our countries don't reflect economic realities," Tuma said Monday, adding that regional central banks have agreed to continue to coordinate their future statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro/forint ended 295.20 Monday after the concerted verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the central banks, versus 299.90 around midday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS - The three-year bond yield was at 13.25% late Monday from 13.65% late Friday; five-year was at 12.15% from 12.85%; 10-year was at 11.45% from 12%. \par  \par POLISH BOND YIELDS - The two-year average yield was 5.65% late Monday from 5.75% Friday; 5-year at 5.81% from 5.93%; 10-year at 6.10% from 6.19%.\par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD - 10-year benchmark at 4.84% late Monday from 4.90% late Friday. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian bonds Tuesday should stay at new levels after a rally Monday cut yields and drove up prices across the board tracking forint gains. Short-end yields dropped 30 basis points, yields on the long-end fell 20 basis points but activity is far from busy, a trader said, adding that domestic buyers were probably behind the move.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish bonds are also set to stabilize after their prices shot up in thin trade Monday as commercial banks placed excess liquidity in treasury bonds. The move was spurred by Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capping the sale of its own bills Friday in order to stimulate lending, a dealer said, adding that bonds should stay flat until Wednesday when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holds its interest rate decision.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20                    1365.16   loss   17.9   dn 1.3% \par BUX                     10263.06   loss  117.4   dn 1.1% \par PX                        645.1    loss    3.1   dn 0.5% \par RTS - Russian markets closed Monday for a national holiday \par DJ Stoxx EU Enlarged 15: 1186.75 gain 4.07 up 0.3% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: The mood in Warsaw Tuesday hinges entirely on U.S. markets, as early gains Monday didn't last but evaporated amid weak sentiment towards financial stocks and concerns that U.S. indexes will head below November lows, a local analyst said. PKO BP ended down 3.4%; commodities stocks bucking the trend helped KGHM close up 2.4%, aided by copper prices and a weak zloty.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Selloffs that started late Monday are likely to continue Tuesday unless U.S. markets end flat or higher Monday, a Prague-based trader said. NWR, ending down 0.8%, and Telefonica O2 CR, down 0.5%, will be in the spotlight Tuesday when they release their FY 2008 results. Zentiva, ending down 0.6%, may gain Tuesday after getting a ratings upgrade late Monday. CEZ emerged from the selloff late Monday to close up 1.2%, as investors defend the CZK680 level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: The market expects no surprises from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which ended down 2.8%, when it reports earnings Tuesday morning, but MOL's earnings, due later this week, could be more exciting, a trader said. MOL ended up 2.5%. "Everything has been built into MOL by now, only very weak numbers could pull it down," a trader said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 OTP Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  suffers with peers, closed down 5.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN MOSCOW: Russian markets closed Monday for a national holiday [23-09 2302ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Eastern European nations must speak with one voice to refuse protectionism, Poland's foreign minister said Monday amid tension about state aid offered by western EU states to industry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: The National Bank of Poland (NBP) called on other central and eastern European central banks on Monday to coordinate efforts in countering the dramatic devaluation of the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The recent weakening of the Czech koruna and other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been exaggerated, prompting central and eastern European central banks to step up their cooperation, the governor of the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday. And the recent weakening of the Czech korunamay force the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lift interest rates, the governor added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: EU ministers agreed Monday that Russia and Turkey could take part in some meetings of the bloc's planned "Eastern Partnership," aimed at boosting ties with six former Soviet states.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA: Russian billionaire Pyotr Aven is hoping to become Latvia's new prime minister, his adviser said on Monday, a proposal that could stir resentment in a former Soviet republic once ruled from Moscow. Aven, who is of Latvian origin, is the chairman of top Russian lender \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and has "rich experience" that could help the recession-battered Baltic state, his adviser Alexander Gafin said on Echo of Moscow radio station.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to act to prevent excessive forint weakness and volatility, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday as part of a coordinated verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the central banks of Central and Eastern Europe.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 HUNGARY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  Nyrt.'s (MTA) fourth-quarter net profit rose more than analysts had expected as an increase in earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization and a drop in depreciation and amortization expenses were only partly offset by higher income taxes and net financial costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: The European Bank for Reconstruction and Development is considering a senior loan for up to EUR100 million to Romanian lender \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banca Comerciala Romana SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  to be lent by BCR to small and medium enterprises in Romania, EBRD said Monday, news agency Mediafax reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: The leu's current exchange rate is sustainable, Romania's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Mugur Isarescu said Monday, news agency Mediafax reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Sean Carney, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 191 375, sean.carney@dowjones.com (Margit Feher in Budapest and Malgorzata Halaba & Marek Strzelecki in Warsaw contributed to this briefing.) [ 02-23-09 2302ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020090224e52o0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
