\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 4-Zloty sinks to 4-1/2 yr low as  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  continues slide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dagmara Leszkowicz and Jason Hovet   \par \uc2 953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2009\par 16:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds forint, crown rebounds)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW/PRAGUE, Feb 4 (Reuters) - Fleeing foreign investors drove the Polish zloty to its lowest since June 2004 on Wednesday, and central Europe's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed weak under the weight of recession fears and a collapse in fund flows.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian forint rebounded from a record low of 304 per euro, which some dealers said may have been the result of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the unit.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined comment. [ID:nL4580034]\par \par \uc2 The zloty <EURPLN=> dropped 0.9 percent to 4.676 per euro by 1442 GMT, cutting some losses but keeping pressure on bonds. The forint was bid at 297.2, up 0.7 percent from Tuesday. Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have lost about 11 percent already in 2009.\par \par \uc2 "The fate of the zloty is definitely in the hands of foreign investors as domestic ones are completely inactive," a Warsaw-based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Steep manufacturing drops are signalling a rough landing for the region's export-heavy economies. That has spooked investors who, buffeted by other emerging markets news including a sovereign rating cut for Russia by agency Fitch, have accelerated the outflow of funds from the region.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Rate setters have loosened policy -- Romania cut by 25 basis\par points to 10 percent on Wednesday -- eliminating some of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 premium of the higher-yielding assets, which analysts warn could lead to a collapse in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . [ID:nL4248765\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In both (Hungary and Poland), high degrees of foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denominated debt make the real economic situation worse," Ulrich Leuchtmann, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Frankfurt, wrote on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This way, both countries have an additional driver of a devaluation spiral."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The zloty has lost 27.8 percent and the forint 20.9 percent since mid-2008 -- a stark reversal from the all-time highs central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded at in the summer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the depreciation, Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor said there was no need to prop up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . [ID:nWAR007878]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some dealers suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market. "We have heard from market talk that a German bank, which is not a regular player in the Hungarian market, sold Swiss francs against forints. Dealers believe this is an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," one Budapest-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said the excessive volatility of the forint was harmful. The governor said the bank had the appropriate tools to dampen excessive swings. [ID:nL3409512]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW PRESSURES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ratings agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Wednesday it was comfortable with Hungary's A3 debt status at the moment, but the country's economic outlook and large foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denominated private debt posed a risk. [ID:nL496524]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch cut emerging market giant Russia's rating to BBB from BBB+ on Wednesday, adding to emerging Europe woes as the move puts pressure on the region's reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Romania, the leu <EURRON=> was down a touch at 4.302 to the euro after the first cut in interest rates in 19 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It (the cut) is not going to have much of an impact on overall liquidity levels. Market interest rates don't bear much resemblance to the policy rate at present," said James Lord, strategist at Standard Bank in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech crown <EURCZK=> rebounded to 28.225 to the euro on profit-taking before a rate decision on Thursday, with expectations of a half percentage point cut.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT-------------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                                            today    in 2009 \par Czech crown      <EURCZK=>   28.225   28.477  +0.89%    -5.22%\par Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>    4.641    4.598  -0.93%    -11.33%\par Hungarian forint <EURHUF=>  297.2    299.24   +0.69%    -11.32%\par Croatian kuna    <EURHRK=>    7.4      7.381  -0.26%    -0.47%\par Romanian leu     <EURRON=>    4.302    4.299  -0.07%    -6.69%\par Serbian dinar    <EURRSD=>   92.55    94.24   +1.83%    -3.32%\par Yield Spreads\par Czech treasury bonds <0#CZBMK=