\par\par \b \uc2 FOCUS: Southeast Europe Begins To Wobble On Banking Woes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Emsden and Adam Cohen   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2009\par 16:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Southeastern Europe's sputtering economies face growing trouble as their foreign-owned banks are increasingly unable to provide financing.\par \par \uc2 The European Commission Thursday dashed hopes that collective aid might be channeled to the region's banks, saying that the lenders themselves should rely on the national bank-rescue plans of their home countries to help foreign subsidiaries.\par \par \uc2 "The rescue plans that have been adopted throughout Europe" by national governments "are for the (banking) groups as a whole," including foreign divisions, the commission's spokeswoman on economic issues, Amelia Torres, told a news briefing in Brussels.\par \par \uc2 Big euro-zone banks, including Italy's \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  SpA (UCG.MI) and Austria's Raiffeissen International (RIBH.VI), that control 90% of banking in Eastern Europe had appealed for help from the European Union and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter growing liquidity problems at their emerging-market franchises.\par \par \uc2 Similar troubles have been tackled in wealthier countries by large-scale government guarantees, but poorer countries can ill afford such measures, especially if they go to bail out wealthy western shareholders.\par \par \uc2 The commission statement came after the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development this week lowered its forecast for 2009 economic growth in eastern Europe and the former Soviet Union to 0.1% from 2.5% previously.\par \par \uc2 While Romania and Bulgaria are not expected to suffer a formal recession, they face the sharpest slowdowns after especially rapid expansions. That growth was fueled by a surge in credit coming from the euro area and destined to finance a consumption and investment spree.\par \par \uc2 Romania and Bulgaria now have large current account deficits and, if access to credit dries up, will soon be looking for institutional aid from the International Monetary Fund, local bankers warn.\par \par \uc2 Most of the lenders are owned by euro-zone banks that are now receiving state aid, usually targeted at propping up their balance sheets and supporting domestic activities rather than cross-border empires.\par \par \uc2 "Bank lending to the (Eastern Europe) region is likely to be very subdued," the Institute of International Finance, an umbrella group of the world's largest banks, said in a report this week.\par \par \uc2 Financial flows to emerging Europe - many of them loans between banking divisions - amounted to 13% of GDP in 2007 but will be only 1% in 2009, the group said, adding that "such a swing is unprecedented in scale."\par \par \uc2 Bulgaria's current account deficit, reflecting its reliance on finance from abroad, rose to 25% of gross domestic product in the 11 months through November.\par \par \uc2 That gap, coming at a time when property prices and foreign direct investment are falling fast and total retail lending has begun to contract, means the economy is "facing a very hard landing, especially if policy makers want to retain the existing fixed-exchange rate" between the lev and the euro, said Timothy Ash, chief emerging-markets strategist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A junior party in Bulgaria's coalition this week urged Sofia to negotiate a standby loan with the IMF.\par \par \uc2 In Romania, the government has repeatedly excluded any deal with the IMF, which has a delegation visiting the country this week. That resistance is likely to crumble soon, as the new government struggles to find cash to pay the salary hikes it championed ahead of last November's election, a local banker said.\par \par \uc2 Private-sector credit growth has slowed to practically zero since October, down from annual rates of more than 50% just last summer.\par \par \uc2 With a budget deficit of more than 5% of GDP last year and growing risks it will rise further this year, Romania is seeking around EUR7 billion in institutional aid, preferably from the E.U. rather than the IMF, President Traian Basescu told national media earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 "The state may find it impossible to pay present salaries and pensions," said Ilie Serbanescu, an economist in Bucharest and former cabinet minister.\par \par \uc2 Romania's National Employment Agency this week doubled its forecast job losses in the first three months of 2009 to 60,000. In Bulgaria, the jobless rate began moving up in December and business confidence fell to a new 10-year low in January.\par \par \uc2 Euro-zone banks with businesses in the region wanted the E.U. to prod the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into helping, possibly in the form of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps similar to those already arranged for Poland and \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "An \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along the lines of a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap, won't cost as much for new member states as for a big euro-zone country," said \uc2 Unicredit\uc2 's chief spokesman. "It's about efficiency."\par \par \uc2 -By Christopher Emsden, Dow Jones Newswires; +39-02-58-21-99-05; chris.emsden@dowjones.com (Adam Cohen in Brussels contributed to this report.) [ 29-01-09 1559GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5008   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090129e51t000xo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
