\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 4-Hungary's forint leads  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  up after rate cut\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jason Hovet   \par \uc2 906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2008\par 18:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Serbia's dinar \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , details)\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, Nov 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forint jumped on Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised markets with an interest rate cut, as central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks rose due to an uptick in sentiment for emerging assets.\par \par \uc2 The forint <EURHUF=> led gainers with a 2.5 percent rise from Friday's levels to bid at 261.3 per euro by 1633 GMT. It shot up 1.5 percent to a two-week high after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly cut borrowing rates by 50 basis points to 11.0 percent. (For details please double click on [ID:nLO117699])\par \par \uc2 Analysts had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold fire on rates until the start of next year, after it raised them by 300 bps last month to prop up the then floundering forint.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about Hungarian banks' exposure to foreign credit had hammered markets in October, pushing the government to get help from the International Monetary Fund and European Union.\par \par \uc2 "Some people might think, now with the rescue package of the IMF and EU, all is over and Hungary is turning back to normal," said Ulrich Leuchtmann, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in Frankfurt, adding that a thin market had added to gains. "In the medium-term, these people that are getting bullish on (the forint) might prove to be wrong."\par \par \uc2 Hungarian bond yields slipped following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move.\par \par \uc2 Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were stronger than Friday's levels, along with central Europe's bourses, which added more than 6 percent on a rally due to a rescure plan for embattled \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Poland's zloty <EURPLN=> added 1.2 percent to 3.84 per euro, Romania's leu <EURRON=> rose 0.7 percent to 3.79 per euro, and the Czech crown <EURCZK=> gained 1.4 percent to 25.37 per euro.\par \par \uc2 But the Serb dinar <EURRSD=>, after slipping to record lows last week, lost 1.1 percent to 88.83 per euro, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has spent 471 million euros since Oct. 1 to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  [ID:nLO130925].\par \par \uc2 TONE CHANGE\par \par \uc2 Dealers kept a cautious tone for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have fallen sharply from record highs in July as the global financial crisis hit the region in October and economies slow in the face of waning euro zone demand.\par \par \uc2 Hungary is expected to fall into recession next year, and other countries are likely to have their growth rates halved.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Moody's\uc2  analyst said on Monday that Romania, one of the region's fastest growers, may enter recession next year due to worsening global conditions [ID:nLO697678].\par \par \uc2 "The overall picture lies with risk aversion," said Miroslav Tutter, a trader with Prague's \uc2 Komercni Banka\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Czech and Slovak central banks have already cut borrowing costs in the face of the global slowdown, while Serbia followed Hungary last month with a sharp rise to protect the dinar.\par \par \uc2 Declining \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are seen forcing central banks in Romania and Poland, which meet this week, to hold rates.\par \par \uc2 Markets in Poland, though, have priced in rate cuts ahead of Wednesday's policy meeting, dealers said, and bond yields fell on Monday, helped by sentiment on stocks.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT-------------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                                            today    in 2008 \par Czech crown      <EURCZK=>   25.37    25.719  +1.36%   +4.25%\par Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>    3.844    3.891  +1.21%   -6.76%\par Hungarian forint <EURHUF=>  261.33   268.00   +2.49%   -3.35%\par Croatian kuna    <EURHRK=>    7.12     7.126  +0.08%   +2.82%\par Romanian leu     <EURRON=>    3.786    3.815  +0.76%   -5.75%\par Serbian dinar    <EURRSD=>   88.837   87.838  -1.14%   -12.79%\par Yield Spreads\par Czech treasury bonds <0#CZBMK=>\par 3-yr T-bond   CZ3YT=RR    0 basis points to  165bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond   CZ5YT=RR   +8 basis points to  +146bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond   CZ9YT=RR   +7 basis points to  +85bps over bmk*\par Polish treasury bonds <0#PLBMK=>\par 2-yr T-bond   PL2YT=RR    -11 basis points to  +407bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond   PL5YT=RR    -13 basis points to  +347bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond PL10YT=RR    -10 basis points to  +286bps over bmk*\par Hungarian treasury bonds <0#HUBMK=>\par 3-yr T-bond   HU3YT=RR    -50 basis points to +1017bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond   HU5YT=RR    -23 basis points to  +962bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond   HU10YT=RR  -29 basis points to  +578bps over bmk*\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *Benchmark is German bond equivalent. All data taken from Reuters at 1735 CET. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  percent change calculated from the daily d