\par\par \b \uc2 Hungary needs speedy euro adoption, deep spending cuts -banker\b0\par\par\uc2 371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2008\par 22:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, Oct 26 (Reuters) - Drastic spending cuts, tax cuts for companies and speedy euro adoption are needed for Hungary to climb out of the global financial crisis and regain investor confidence, the head of the country's biggest bank said.\par \par \uc2 Sandor Csanyi, chief executive of \uc2 OTP Bank\uc2  , said that the forint's sharp fall last week was fundamentally unjustified and the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from further drops.\par \par \uc2 "Rapid euro adoption is the only way (to regain investor confidence)," Csanyi told state television MTV on Sunday. "I think Hungarian politicians need to come together and seek an exception from EU rules to adopt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as soon as possible."\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks were one of the hardest hit in the 27 nation European Union over the past two weeks on concern about the country's high debt level, heavy reliance on external financing and the proliferation of household foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans.\par \par \uc2 "The forint must be protected, if necessary, through further rate hikes or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Csanyi, who is also one of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's richest men.\par \par \uc2 "I think that the forint's realistic value is around 270 (but) for the next few months, I could even accept 280 as that would really help the economy and exporting firms," Csanyi said.\par \par \uc2 The forint fell to an all time low of 285 versus the euro on Thursday before recovering to close the week around 280, nearly 11 percent weaker since the start of the year. The forint is among the worst performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 To protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week raised its benchmark interest rate to 11.5 percent from 8.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say Hungary will adopt the euro around 2014.\par \par \uc2 Csanyi added that to stabilise the forint and meet euro adoption criteria, the country needs to drastically reduce spending and cut taxes for corporations while reducing the budget deficit to a range of 0 to 1 percent of GDP from current plans for a 3.4 percent deficit this year.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Balazs Koranyi; Editing by Phil Berlowitz)\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY-EURO|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081026e4aq000oa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
