\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Has CEE Hit Bottom? Some Say Yes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2008\par 05:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 1200/1000 Poland, 52-Wk T-bill tender, yield at previous auction 6.295% \par 1230/1030 German Chancellor Angela Merkel in Prague, meeting with Czech Prime \par           Minister Mirek Topolanek to discuss bilateral relations and \par           European affairs \par 1400/1200 Hungary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy meeting, market expects no change to \par           8.5% key rate \par 1600/1400 Polish petrochemical company PKN Orlen and Czech unit \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unipetrol AS\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par           publish selected 3Q operating data, executive comment \par N/A       European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell, Czech \par           \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Zdenek Tuma speak in Prague at conference on \par           financial services \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PRAGUE (Dow Jones)--Central Europe's markets are ready to recover with some analysts saying the region has hit bottom after investors last week liquidated positions in banks and utilities on fears that Hungary's economic crisis would spill over its borders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "There have been huge sellers throughout the region, it looks like international accounts totally cashed out of CEE banks, switching from Eastern Europe to Western Europe," Wood & Co trader Filip Kejla said. "We should go higher in the new week."\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds a monetary policy meeting Monday, though the market expects no change to interest rates, as policy makers are seen giving time for last week's European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take effect.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no plans to use the credit line offered by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Hungary last week, the Wall Street Journal quoted Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Andras Simor as saying.\par \par \uc2 In Poland, markets will be watching the government for confirmation of reports that it's planning to enter the European Union's ERM2 exchange rate corridor as early as March of next year. Euro strategy is supposed to be on the agenda of a special cabinet meeting Monday.\par \par \uc2 Regional oil refiner PKN Orlen and its Czech unit, Unipetrol, are expected to issue guidance on third-quarter operating results after the markets close Monday.\par \par \uc2 But investors in Prague are wary of putting money down until they see Hungary get back on its feet. That poor sentiment left the Prague Stock Exchange at its lowest level in five years on Friday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 24.915-65  24.818-78  +0.39  24.925  24.857   -5.97 \par EUR/HUF 264.95-45  264.95-45   0.00  Untraded today   +4.79 \par EUR/PLN 3.5493-23  3.5498-18  -0.01  3.5493  3.5523   -1.42 \par EUR/SKK 30.450-90  30.455-95  -0.02  30.455  30.490   -9.35 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's euro/zloty cross settled late Friday around PLN3.55, right in the middle of its recent PLN3.48-PLN3.62 trading range, but any technical support is theoretical as the unit is capable of 3% intraday swings as global risk aversion and regional sentiment fluctuate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech koruna weakened to a six-month low Friday on expectations the Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will cut rates at its next policy meeting. But late in Friday's session the koruna recovered. Monday euro/koruna is seen trading between 25.0 and 25.1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par HUNGARIAN BOND YIELDS - the three-year benchmark bond yield was at 12.06% late Friday from 12.00% late Thursday; the five-year yield was at 11.81% from 11.50%, the 10-year was at 9.65% from 10.00%. \par  \par POLISH BOND YIELDS - the average yield on two-year paper was at 6.31% late Friday from 6.42% Thursday; five-year was at 6.54% from 6.42%; 10-year was at 6.51% from 6.44%.\par  \par CZECH BOND YIELD - the 10-year benchmark was 4.50% late Friday from 4.30% late Thursday. \par  \par    In Budapest, the epicenter of fear in central Europe's markets, traders expect some buying activity to return this week as a result of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures to kickstart trading. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "These are significant steps (from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) but time is needed for obvious results," one trader noted, adding that selling pressure frmo foreign investors is subsiding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Warsaw, short-term bond prices moved higher, supported by prospects for rate cuts as the economy weakens, although longer maturities have been hurt by problems in Hungary. Traders said the market lacks liquidity and yields will be under pressure near-term if the situation does not improve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG20    1779.09        loss   120.59  dn  6.4% \par BUX      12928.7        loss   310.69  dn  2.3% \par PX         842.8        loss    95.7   dn  10% \par DJ Stoxx Enlarged 15 1866.61 loss 103.97 dn 5.3% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: Investors closed positions on banks across the region, as Poland and the Czech Republic suffer guilt-by-association due to Hungary's woes. Analysts said that this looks like the last stage of the bank sell-off, and a strong rebound should be in the offing. Friday, Pekao closed 10.7% lower, PKO BP lost 7.7%, BZ WBK down 6.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Investors liquidated positions in banking and utilities last week, and some traders say the market looks to be at the bottom, so gains can be expected starting on Monday. On Friday, NWR lost 23%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Komercni Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 17%, Erste Bank dropped 16%, and Unipetrol surrendered 12%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: "It could be the light at the end of the tunnel that there was significant buying interest during the last 30 minutes Friday, but direction is still highly uncertain," a trader said. OTP recovered from double-digit losses amid massive volumes to end 4.5% lower, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  was down 2.3% and Richter fell 7.0%. [ 10-19-08 2328ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: After threatening to veto the European Union's climate package, Poland is expected to table its conditions for agreement at a meeting of EU environment ministers in Luxembourg on Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech Prime Minister Mirek Topolanek's party suffered heavy losses to the opposition Social Democrats (CSSD) in regional elections, official results showed Saturday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The Hungarian government is in talks with the country's commercial banks on a scheme to cap the monthly repayments on retail \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -based loans so popular among Hungarians, Prime Minister Ferenc Gyurcsany said Saturday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The adoption of the common European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro, would help Hungary tackle the loss of investor confidence it has been facing because of the global financial market crisis, \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2  Governor Andras Simor said Saturday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's budget deficit will be below the government's recently reduced target of 3.4% of gross domestic product, Finance Minister Janos Veres said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Portuguese energy company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Energias de Portugal SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (EDP.LB), or EDP, has bought 85% stakes in Romanian wind power companies Renovatio Power SRL and Cernavoda Power SRL for EUR8.4 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Sean Carney, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 191 375, sean.carney@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Edith Balazs in Budapest and David McQuaid & Marek Strzelecki in Warsaw contributed to the briefing.) [ 10-19-08 2329ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020081020e4ak0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
