\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Hungary Simor:Will Defend Forint If Weakness Persists\b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2008\par 12:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds further details.)\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  is ready to defend the Hungarian forint with all the policy tools it has to hand, as the sharp weakening of the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unfounded by the country's improving economic fundamentals, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Andras Simor said Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The interest rate is our main policy tool, and that's in the competency of the Monetary Policy Council," Simor told a small group of reporters on the sidelines of a press conference held with Finance Minister Janos Veres.\par \par \uc2 When asked whether the MPC should act outside its ordinary schedule, Simor said: "In extraordinary situations I don't exclude anything. We will see what will happen, where the market goes, and we will react to that."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready "to use its policy tools at hand to reach a forint level that's appropriate for the macroeconomic (conditions), the country's stability, the future low inflation. We'll decide what tools to use if this market situation persists," Simor said.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  target, but has an inflation rate target, Simor added. Nevertheless, it keeps a constant eye on the forint moves since that's a major factor which affects inflation, he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the bond market, Simor added. \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government bond market came to a virtual halt Thursday on a liquidity shortage.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will launch from Monday a daily, one-day euro/forint swap tender to boost short-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity, Simor said. The bank will act as an intermediary in the tender between the commercial banks. Besides that, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at the service of the commercial banks to provide unlimited forint liquidity as usual, Simor said.\par \par \uc2 The capital market weakness seen Thursday in Hungary is unjustified by Hungary's economic fundamentals, Simor said. The current account deficit has narrowed and will continue to shrink, the budget deficit has narrowed considerably and the external trade indicator has also improved. Furthermore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  welcomed the government's commitment to reducing the budget deficit to 3.2% of gross domestic product next year while revising the main economic assumptions in light of the global crisis, Simor said. This year's budget deficit could be "significantly below" the government's original target for a deficit of 4% of GDP, Simor added.\par \par \uc2 The amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans, while are relatively high, pose no risk to the country's financial market stability, contrary to some analysts' views, Simor stressed. That's because 65% of the banks' foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt are loans from their international parents, and as such acts a a quasi working capital-type financing, Simor said.\par \par \uc2 -By Margit Feher, Dow Jones Newswires; +36-20-925-2364; margit.feher@dowjones.com [ 10-10-08 1029GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081010e4aa000jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
