\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Hungary's June trade data signals growth risks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sandor Peto   \par \uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2008\par 09:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Recasts with further comments, background)\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Hungary posted a smaller-than-expected trade surplus in June, adding to signs that economic slowdown in the euro zone is taking its toll on central European exports, a key growth driver, analysts said.\par \par \uc2  The surplus amounted to 100.3 million euros <HUTRD=ECI> in June, according to preliminary data published by the Central Statistics Office (KSH) on Friday, up from 27.5 million euros in May, but below analysts' 146 million euro forecast <HUEURO1>.\par \par \uc2  Analysts said the figure still did not cause a big surprise after an unexpected 0.3 percent fall in industrial output in June.\par \par \uc2  "The whole CEE (Central European) region is starting to print weaker growth and worse trade numbers given easing external demand and surging commodity prices," said Silja Sepping of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2  In the first half of the year, Hungary's exports were up 11.9 percent while import growth was only 10.0 percent year on year. In June alone, imports were up by 8.4 percent year-on-year and exports rose by only 7.6 percent.\par \par \uc2  Gergely Suppan, analyst at Takarekbank said slower export dynamics was partly caused by a base effect as the sale of the European Union's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grains stocks from \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  added some 600 million euros to exports last year.\par \par \uc2  "The other cause is the slowdown in euro zone which according to more an more signs also affects the Czechs and will affect Slovakia even though it may not be visible there due to new production capacities," he said.\par \par \uc2  "The Czech, Slovakian and Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed much more than the forint in the past years and this indicates that the problem in Hungary is low investment activity (in the past years) rather than the strong forint," he added. \par \par \uc2  NO RATE CUT IN SHORT TERM\par \par \uc2  Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit record highs last month amid expectations for interest rate hikes in the region, triggering complaints from exporters.\par \par \uc2  The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the first in the region on Thursday to reverse earlier rate hikes, delivering a surprise 25 basis point cut in its key repo rate to 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2  Analysts said the last Hungarian data confirmed that weakening demand in the euro zone posed a risk to forecasts for Hungarian economic growth around two percent this year which would be among the lowest among new European Union members.\par \par \uc2  Worries over competitiveness also maintain political pressure in Hungary and the liberal opposition party said on Thursday that the minority Socialist government must step down if fails to present a programme including tax cuts soon.\par \par \uc2  The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still unlikely to launch interest rate cuts in the next months so as to help the economy as it is assessing existing inflation risks, analysts said.\par \par \uc2  "They will not rush into cuts soon," said Orsolya Nyeste, analyst at Erste Bank.\par \par \uc2  (Reporting by Sandor Peto/Gergely Szakacs; Editing by Victoria Main)  \par \par \uc2 HUNGARY-TRADE/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|HX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080808e4880014y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
