\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Czech Confidence Vote In Focus  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,018 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2007\par 06:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 0900/0800 Hungary Jan-Nov Real Wages\par  \par 0900/0800 Czech Nov Retail \par           Seen +6.3% Y-Y vs +8.6% Y-Y \par  \par 1400/1300 Poland Dec Indus Output \par           Seen +7.9% Y-Y vs +11.7% Y-Y in Nov \par  \par 1400/1300 Poland Dec PPI \par           Seen +3.0% Y-Y vs +2.6% Y-Y in Nov \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.8526-76  3.8506-56  +0.05  3.8591  3.8487   +0.52 \par EUR/HUF 251.70-25  251.65-05  +0.02  251.85  251.90   +0.11 \par EUR/CZK 27.735-65  27.760-90  -0.09  27.775  27.765   +0.89 \par EUR/SKK 34.620-60  34.620-60   0.00  34.650  34.560   +0.51 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: Focus Friday will be on Polish inflation data, due at 1300 GMT. Longer term, "we continue to expect the zloty to appreciate in the next 12 months to PLN3.60 to the euro on the back of the strong external balance," said Goldman Sachs analyst Istvan Zsoldos. Foreign direct investment inflow is set to continue to more than cover the total current account deficit, thus supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Zsoldos. Others are less bullish, however: BPH Bank forecasts the euro firming to PLN3.95 in the medium term.  \par \par \uc2 The zloty strengthened against the euro and the dollar Thursday after rating agency Fitch upgraded Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating to "A-", citing strong macroeconomic performance and an improvement in the country's budget situation. The euro began the day with a test of resistance at PLN3.91, but by the end of the session had retreated to trade in its recent PLN3.8550-PLN3.91 range. Late Thursday, the euro was trading at PLN3.8615, from PLN3.8923 late Wednesday. The dollar was at PLN2.9801, from PLN3.0080.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT: Opinions are divided over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's outlook. Calyon Bank expects the euro to remain in its current HUF252-HUF255 range in the short term. Only Hungary has the potential for even higher interest rates in Central and Eastern Europe, which makes the forint the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of choice in the region, said Calyon. However, one trader sees the current firming of the forint as "a short-term correction" and expects it to weaken, with the euro trading at around HUF260 by the spring. Brown Brothers Harriman strategist Meg Browne also said the forint could suffer in coming months. "The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains hawkish, but the rate hiking cycle has largely run its course and the market will begin anticipating rate cuts, creating another risk for the forint," Browne said.  \par \par \uc2 The forint strengthened against the euro Thursday, boosted by Fitch's upgrade of Poland's credit rating. Late Thursday, the euro was trading at HUF251.65, from HUF252.65 early Thursday and from HUF254.30 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA: Domestic investors are likely to adopt a "wait and see" attitude as they await the results of the government confidence vote in parliament, scheduled for Friday, Calyon Bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The koruna firmed a touch against the euro Thursday in what traders called technical trade. A Raiffeisenbank trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported mainly by foreign buyers in London. "There's no fundamental change, only technical trading," he said. The koruna had weakened earlier in the week after a member of the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy board downplayed the chances of an interest rate rise in the near future. Late Thursday, the euro was trading at CZK27.85, from CZK27.87.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: "Regarding the inflation outlook, we can say that it is getting better and better," said Calyon Bank in a note. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to continue monetary tightening in the first quarter," the bank said. Calyon said January inflation data will be key to whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will reverse its tightening policy in the near term with a rate cut.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Polish government bonds strengthened Thursday, tracking the zloty and local stocks, with a late added boost from Fitch's upgrade. Yields fell across the curve. Late Thursday, the average yield on the two-year bond was at 4.27%, from 4.35% before the Fitch upgrade, and from 4.39% late Wednesday. The five-year yield was at 4.88%, from 4.89%, while the 10-year was at 5.11%, from 5.18%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: Focus Friday will be on Hungarian real wage data, due at 0800 GMT. One trader sees the chance for a continuation of the positive correction Friday on the long end of the curve, but said markets are very hectic. "Essentially, bonds are moving in a 20-30 basis point range and I see no breakout at this point," he said. Longer term, Goldman Sachs analyst Zsoldos sees scope for the country to undershoot both its first-quarter and yearly deficit targets. The Hungarian government seems to be very cautious when planning as much as 65.5% of the full-year budget deficit target to be generated in the first quarter, said Zsoldos. Dresdner Kleinwort said the combination of overweight investor positions, risk of fiscal slippage and vulnerability to the global environment could be enough to trigger a significant correction in the Hungarian debt curve. Risks could increase in the event of a euro-zone economic slowdown, the bank said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian government bonds were mixed Thursday in hectic trade. Yields fell at the long end of the curve after Fitch's upgrade of Poland's credit rating. U.S. inflation data also leant support to the longer end of the curve. Late Thursday, the average yield on three-year paper was at 7.75%, from 7.60% late Wednesday, while the five-year average yield was at 7.44%, from 7.50%, and the 10-year at 7.06%, from 7.10%. Hungary auctioned 50 billion forints ($256.53 million) of five-year bonds and HUF40 billion forints of 10-year bonds Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS: Local investors are expected to wait for the result of the government confidence vote in parliament, scheduled for Friday, before taking new positions in the bond market. Some traders said a "yes" vote could push yields a little higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Czech government bonds weakened a touch Thursday in calm trade. Late Thursday, the average yield on the benchmark 10-year bond was at 3.87%, from 3.84%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   STOCKS \par  \par WIG20          3437.89      gain   115.28  up 3.5% \par BUX           24172.51      gain   116.71  up 0.5% \par PX             1624.20      gain    14.30  up 0.9% \par Enlarged 15    3180.08      gain   74.56   up 2.4% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW: Following strong gains Thursday, a "light correction is possible to catch a breath, but the potential for gains seems immense," a trader said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WIG20 index ended up 3.5% at 3437.89 after a session of much larger than average volumes as the Polish Finance Ministry's forecast for strong fourth-quarter gross domestic product attracted foreign buyers who had been sidelined for the past few weeks. Bank stocks were very strong, with PKO BP ending up 4.3% and Pekao SA up 5.2%. KGHM ended up 4.7% after the company released solid guidance for 2007. PKN Orlen ended up 2.6% as the dismissal of the company's chief executive lifted the uncertainty that has weighed on the stock. Bucking the trend, TPSA ended down 0.4%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST: The outlook for Friday is murky, with the direction of the BUX index likely to depend heavily on the mood on international stock markets, a trader said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BUX ended up 0.5% at 24172.51 Thursday in average-volume trade, with morning optimism waning by midday. Drug stocks came under heavy selling pressure following a Citigroup downgrade of Richter, which ended down 1.0%, while Egis was down 0.6%. Among other stocks, OTP ended up 0.4%, MOL up 1.3% and Magyar Telekom up 1.3%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE: Looking to Friday and next week the performance of Czech stocks will also depend on how well major markets do, especially the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PX index ended up 0.9% at 1624.2 Thursday, with most stocks ending higher, fueled by similar gains across the region, with some rebounding from recent losses. The market was led by Telefonica O2 CR, which ended up 1.7%. "Telefonica is up on some solid demand from overseas investors," a Patria Finance trader said. CEZ ended up 1.3%, rebounding from losses Wednesday. Fueled by the same positive mood, Zentiva and Komercni Banka ended up 1.6% and 1.3%, respectively. Of the more actively-traded stocks, only Erste Bank bucked the trend, ending down 0.4%, largely on losses in Vienna, where the stock is also traded. [ 01-19-07 0052ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's biggest oil refiner and retailer, PKN Orlen, fired its CEO on Thursday. Igor Chalupec was removed by a majority vote of the company managing board. He was replaced by his deputy since October, Piotr Kownacki, 52, who is close to President Lech Kaczynski. The company said the change was made on request of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Treasury Ministry\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is the largest single shareholder in Orlen with 28 percent of its shares. The state controls the supervisory board.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Russia's state-controlled gas monopoly OAO Gazprom called Thursday for more talks with its Polish counterpart Polskie Gornictwo Naftowe i Gazownictwo, or PGNiG, in their dispute over transit fees on a major pipeline that carries Russian gas to Europe. Stressing what it called PGNiG's uncompromising approach to the dispute, Gazprom said its delegates to the board of EuRoPol Gaz, the joint venture company that owns the Polish stretch of the Yamal-Europe pipeline, "offered to continue the work on the questions mentioned in a format of bilateral consultations."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Coal miners in southern Poland on Thursday voted on whether to stage a strike in protest at restructuring plans, a move the government said would be a costly mistake. The government's restructuring plans call for tying earnings to mines' profitability and allowing privatization of individual mines.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: An investment agency sponsored by the Czech government helped bring in more than EUR4 billion last year, the trade ministry said Thursday. Deals with foreign and local companies in 2006 set up a total of 176 new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  worth 114.6 billion koruna ($5.5 billion) - up from CZK73 billion the previous year, the Ministry of Industry and Trade said in a statement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The European Union grain management committee agreed Thursday to raise volumes in tenders to resell soft wheat held in German and Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stores back on the domestic market.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 HUNGARY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Losses at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's state-owned air carrier Malev Zrt. (MLV.YY) are bigger than the amount indicated at the start of the privatization talks, David Keresztes, a representative of Malev's suitor Lithuanian air company LAL investiciju valdymas told Hungarian state news agency MTI Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: The owner of a television news channel in Romania was sentenced to two years in prison on Thursday for using false documents to set up a bank. In August 1999, Sorin Ovidiu Vantu used a document falsely stating his assets that allowed him to gain a majority stake in the Investment and Development Bank, according to Bucharest Sector 1 court.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: The trade between Romania and China could register an increase of 35 to 50 percent in the coming years despite the restrictions imposed by the European Union (EU) on some Chinese products, chairman of the Romania-China Chamber of Commerce said on Thursday, Xinhua reported.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Hundreds of land owners gathered Thursday in front of Cabinet headquarters to protests against a European environmentalist program they say would block lucrative investment plans in their regions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Susannah Rodgers, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-208-789-8467; susannah.rodgers@dowjones.com [ 01-19-07 0052ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070119e31j0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
