\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Overnight News  \b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2006\par 06:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: The Ukrainian and Polish premiers said Wednesday that Ukraine's Odessa-Brody pipeline should be extended to the Polish city of Plock and carry Caspian Sea oil to Poland and Europe, a move that could help lessen the continent's reliance on Russian oil.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 POLAND: The chances of the EU opening talks next week on a strengthened partnership with Russia diminished Wednesday as Poland stuck by its veto unless Moscow ends its ban on imports of Polish meat and plant products.  \par \par \uc2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech brewery Budejovicky Budvar NP said Wednesday it has had its trademark registered in China, despite a legal challenge from U.S. beer giant Anheuser-Busch Cos. Inc. (BUD).  \par \par \uc2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Officials are to almost double the number of days that they shut down one of the units of their nuclear plant every year because of problems with the fuel rods, an official said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 SLOVAKIA: Slovakia's nearly double-digit growth in the third quarter has divided economists as to whether the economy is overheating or enjoying an auto industry boom that will provide sustainable strength in the future.  \par \par \uc2 HUNGARY: Hungarian government decided Wednesday to raise pensions and pension-like welfare payments 4% on average from Jan. 1, , costing 101.2 billion forints ($402.7 million).  \par \par \uc2 HUNGARY: The Hungarian government is to appeal to the European Court of Justice against the European Commission's latest corn \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations, a government spokeswoman said Wednesday.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HUNGARY\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's State Debt Management Agency, or AKK, is planning the net issue of 444 billion forints ($2.2 billion) in treasury bonds and bills in the December-February period, state news agency MTI said Wednesday, without naming its sources.  \par \par \uc2 ROMANIA: The president called for a delay in privatizing Romania's utility companies, including natural gas producer Romgaz, to give authorities more time to analyze the effects of past selloffs.  \par \par \uc2 SERBIA: High levels of cancer-causing gases, apparently released by a petrochemical plant, triggered a public alarm system in an industrial city near Belgrade, officials said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Susannah Rodgers, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-208-789-8467; susannah.rodgers@dowjones.com [ 11-16-06 0059ET ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  OTHER NEWS \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH: Finnish Prime Minister Matti Vanhanen will fly to Warsaw this week to try to salvage talks on a political and economic cooperation agreement with Russia, which the Polish government is trying to veto.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The outgoing Czech labor minister unveiled a draft proposal to reform the country's pension system Thursday, saying it was a key step in the reform of public finances.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Given a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it isn't clear whether an an additional interest rate increase will be necessary, Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Zsigmond Jarai said Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: The Slovak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't concerned about current exchange rate level of the koruna, its governor said Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: NATO Secretary-General Jaap de Hoop Scheffer and Romania's President Traian Basescu said Thursday that the Western military alliance could take up additional tasks such as boosting energy security for member states.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Hungary is still worried that a planned gold mine in neighboring Romania could lead to the repeat of an environmental disaster caused by a cyanide leak at a similar mine in 2000, the Hungarian prime minister said Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: The jobless rate in Bulgaria fell to 8.38 percent in October, bringing it closer to levels in the European Union, which Bulgaria is due to join on Jan. 1, the government said Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SERBIA: Sweden's foreign minister said Thursday that a solution for Kosovo needs to be "stable and sustainable" and he urged Serbia's leadership to seek a compromise in international talks on the future of the separatist region.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BALKANS: The presidents of Bosnia, Croatia, Serbia and Montenegro signed an agreement Thursday to step up cooperation in the Balkans, a region still traumatized from war in the 1990s, and join forces to fight organized crime and terrorism.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Susannah Rodgers, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-208-789-8467; susannah.rodgers@dowjones.com [ 11-17-06 0059ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020061116e2bg0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
