\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Hungary forint falls past central parity in band  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Krisztina Than  \par \uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2006\par 10:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with more analyst comments, background)  \par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, June 29 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint fell to new record lows in Thursday trading for the first time, on the weak side of central parity in its 30-percent band, as U.S. rate hike fears and weakness in Slovakia assets hit local markets.  \par \par \uc2 The forint plunged to a new low at 282.90 versus the euro, past central parity at 282.36 in its band in volatile trading and government bond yields jumped by 10-15 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 The forint has been easing all this month but its falls accelerated last week as a lack of confidence in Hungary's economic fundamentals despite a recent fiscal adjustment package exacerbated a fall in appetite for emerging market assets.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on shorter bonds have gone up about 140 basis points this month while long-term yields have risen 85 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 Hungary's high twin budget and current account deficits make the country especially vulnerable to any sudden shifts in investor sentiment, and rate hike expectations have strengthened since last Monday's 25 basis point rate hike by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which markets said was a mistaken and confusingly small move.  \par \par \uc2 "Today's fall I think is due to two things unrelated to Hungary: caution ahead of the Fed (rate) decision and its statement, and there is negative contagion from Slovakia," said Koon Chow, market strategist at CSFB in London.  \par \par \uc2 The crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of neighbouring Slovakia fell on Thursday after the leftist party Smer chose two fringe parties for coalition talks there, raising investor worries that such a coalition would reverse reforms and loosen fiscal discipline.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely step in only in case of more significant movements in the forint, but they said rate hikes had been priced in for the next three months for the bank's regular monthly rate meetings.  \par \par \uc2 They also said the forint's weakening past the central parity was in itself not of special importance.  \par \par \uc2 "If we see that the forint gets detached from the region and moves significantly, like two or three percent in a day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , verbally or otherwise, but more likely with a rate hike," Commerzbank's Barbara Nestor said.  \par \par \uc2 "(Crossing the parity) does not have a huge significance by itself. Technical levels may be connected to it but it is hard to tell because the forint has never been that low," said Tamas Vojnits at OTP.  \par \par \uc2 "Together with the weaker government bond market, it will sooner or later force further interest rate hikes," he added.  \par \par \uc2 RATE HIKE LOOKS UNAVOIDABLE  \par \par \uc2 Hungary, which has failed to address its big fiscal deficits in the past four years, is now facing up to the consequences of delaying much-needed deficit cuts at a time when appetite for riskier assets is declining in the world.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the forint was likely to remain under pressure but the situation in Hungarian markets was not like that of Turkey which suffered a sharp selloff recently, forcing drastic rate hikes by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 CSFB's Chow said \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's case was different from Turkey where most players were long Turkish assets and liquidity collapsed.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not the same thing. In Turkey people were very long and they got it wrong and liquidity collapsed. Who has been long Hungarian assets?" Chow said.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to hike rates as inflation is expected to pick up to 6.0-7.0 percent next year after the government's tax hikes, energy price hikes and due to the weak forint.  \par \par \uc2 "Rates could rise by 100 basis points by year-end, or at least to 7 percent with a high probability," OTP's Vojnits said.  \par \par \uc2 Hungary sold 30 billion forints ($133.4 million) worth of 12-month Treasury bills, 10 billion forints less than planned, at an auction on Thursday, the Government Debt Management Agency (AKK) said.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-HUNGARY-FORINT (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|HX|BNX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060629e26t000lr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
