\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-BIS-Hungary cenbank says more rate rises to come  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Krista Hughes  \par \uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2006\par 09:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (recasts, Adds quotes, reaction)  \par \par \uc2 BASEL, Switzerland, June 26 (Reuters) - Further interest rate rises are needed in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no immediate plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to support the forint, the bank's Vice Governor Henrik Auth said on Monday. Auth told Reuters that last week's 0.25 percentage point rate rise was not enough to head off a likely "inflation shock" from the impact of planned government budget reforms, and given the turbulence in emerging markets globally.  \par \par \uc2 In the longer term, budget cuts worth 350 billion forints ($1.60 billion) this year would cut Hungary's external and budget deficits and boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but would also push up inflation.  \par \par \uc2 "We hope that the fundamentals in the medium term, in the next year and after, improve substantially because of the austerity package," Auth said.  \par \par \uc2 "Taking into account the side effects of the austerity package to inflation plus the repricing of emerging market risks internationally, I think we have to start a rate hike cycle."  \par \par \uc2 Market disappointment with the size of last week's rate rise, which took the key rate to 6.25 percent, was partly behind a slide in the forint last week, which hit record lows against the euro on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Auth said: "It is an option. We are watching (the forint) very carefully." Market sentiment, speculation and turbulence were all taken into account.  \par \par \uc2 But he stressed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  target, said there were no immediate plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 INFLATION SHOCK  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government has said the fiscal cuts would reduce the deficit to 5 percent of GDP in 2007 from 8 this year, but preliminary NBH calculations show they could also boost annual average inflation to 6-7 percent next year and 3.5-4.5 in 2008 from 3.3 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 The government calculates inflation will be 5.0-6.0 percent next year, falling to around 3 percent in 2008.  \par \par \uc2 Auth said although the reforms would have only a temporary direct impact on inflation, and would also slow aggregate demand, the real risk came from potential second-round effects and an upwards impact on inflation expectations.  \par \par \uc2 "This is why we have to react," he said.  \par \par \uc2 But the circumstances were not so critical that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to bring forward its next rate decision from the meeting scheduled for July 24.  \par \par \uc2 How much further rates would rise depended on the impact of the reform package, as well as the international environment, which was less favourable for all emerging markets, he said. Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised rates by 2.25 percentage points on Sunday to help stop a slide in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Auth said he was confident NBH Governor Zsigmond Jarai and the three deputies could convince the majority of external policymakers to raise rates further. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials proposed a 50 basis point hike last week but some of the nine external members wanted rates to be kept unchanged, while the rest said a smaller rise to 6.25 percent would be sufficient.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BIS-HUNGARY (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|HX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060626e26q000g1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
