\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: All Eyes On Polish CPI Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2005\par 07:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  \par 0900/0700 Czech Republic July Retail Sales \par           Seen +4.0% on year vs. +4.4% Jun \par  \par 0900/0700 Czech Republic August PPI \par           Seen +1.2% on year vs. +2.0% \par  \par 1200/1000 Poland Auctions 10-Year T-Bonds \par  \par 1530/1330 Hungary Treasury Schedule For Next Week \par  \par 1600/1400 Poland August CPI \par           Seen +1.5% on the year vs. +1.3% July \par  \par 1600/1400 Poland August M3 \par           Seen +10.3% on year vs. +12.1% July \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/PLN 3.9255-15  3.9227-93  +0.07  3.9275  3.9284   -3.78 \par EUR/HUF 245.58-88  245.58-88   0.00  245.59  245.86   +0.19 \par EUR/CZK 29.265-20  29.275-30  -0.03  29.27   29.32    -3.73 \par EUR/SKK 38.410-60  38.413-62  -0.01  38.410  38.460   -0.79 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 THE POLISH ZLOTY: there should be renewed strength in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the weeks ahead, especially after it broke through key resistance at PLN4.00 recently, says J.P. Morgan. The focus Wednesday will be on consumer price data.  \par \par \uc2 The zloty strengthened early Tuesday after late Monday's downtick prompted by disappointing July current account data, but then came off the day's high in the afternoon. Towards the end of the day it was trading at PLN3.9305 to the euro, from PLN3.9430 late Monday, and at PLN3.2000 to the dollar, from PLN3.2050.  \par \par \uc2 THE HUNGARIAN FORINT should weaken to HUF246 after the slightly better-than-expected CPI data published Tuesday. Also, the sale by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of small amounts of bonds, interpreted by some as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, might damp sentiment towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The better-than-forecast August CPI data briefly boosted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Tuesday but negative sentiment towards the Slovak koruna and the Polish zloty caused it to weaken during the course of the day, a local dealer said. The forint traded at HUF245.57 to the euro in the afternoon, breaking out of the tight HUF244.50-HUF245.00 range of the past few days.  \par \par \uc2 THE CZECH KORUNA could weaken beyond CZK29.40 to the euro Wednesday because of the political instability in neighboring Slovakia, although retail sales and PPI data might have an effect, says Komercni Banka. Otherwise, given this week's current account balance numbers, the koruna should be stronger - the only thing preventing it from strengthening is the negative carry which is restricting foreign investment inflow, says Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2 The Czech koruna weakened to CZK29.305-29.335 to the euro Tuesday from CZK29.19 late Monday, pulled down by a decline in the Slovak koruna, which was rattled by local political uncertainty and talk of early elections there.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH BONDS: All eyes will be on the CPI data Wednesday. The consensus forecast is for an 1.5% increase on the year, up from a 1.3% rise in July.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local treasury bonds were little changed Tuesday despite stronger U.S. Treasurys and a recovery in bunds, as local players held back ahead of the CPI data. The average yield on the two-year paper was flat at 4.28%, while the yield on the five-year paper was down two basis points at 4.56%. The yield on the illiquid 10-year paper rose to 4.63% from 4.62% earlier in the day.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARIAN BONDS: the market will chew on the news of Tuesday's unexpected government T-bond sale in the secondary market, and the fact that the government plans similar sales in the future.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian bond yields rose across the curve after the surprise sale triggered a wave of selling from other investors. This is after an early drop of three to five basis points caused by the CPI data, which came in below expectations Tuesday. Overall, yields finished the day about seven basis points higher. Three-year bond yields stood at 5.54%-5.47% and five- and 10-year bond yields were between 5.53% and 46% midafternoon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH BONDS will follow the koruna, which has weakened against the euro, driven by a selloff in the Slovak koruna on calls for early elections there. "Given the koruna, the market sentiment for (Czech) bonds is and may remain rather negative for now," said a bond trader at Komercni Banka.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early Tuesday local bonds prices continued to drop after heavy selloffs Monday but the market calmed down later in the day, recouping most of the losses. The yield on the 10-year paper was unchanged at 3.28%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par WIG20                     2376.95  loss    2.60  dn 0.1% \par BUX                      22811.84  loss   98.70  dn 0.4% \par PX-D                      3496.7   gain   13.8   up 0.4% \par DJ STOXX EU Enlarged 15   2663.16  loss   10.63  dn 0.4% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN WARSAW, more profit-taking is likely Wednesday given the high valuation of local companies, says a Warsaw trader.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WIG20 ended down 0.1%Tuesday after early gains prompted by investors' relief that PKN Orlen won't buy Turkish company Tupras dissipated as sentiment shifted after the release of U.S. PPI data, said local traders. PKN Orlen, PKO BP and TPSA all closed unchanged. Lotos rose 0.5%, KGHM slipped 0.5%, while Agora rallied 1.2% on a share buyback program. Separately, Poland's gas monopoly PGNiG set the share price for its upcoming initial public offering at PLN2.98 a share.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN BUDAPEST, all eyes will be on MOL following Tuesday's drop in the share price.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BUX slipped 0.4% Tuesday on the back of a large decline in MOL's share price - down 1.4% at HUF23.370 despite the fact that Morgan Stanley increased MOL's target price to HUF26 earlier Tuesday. OTP was flat on the day, while Richter dropped 0.8%, as did Magyar Telekom. The biggest riser was Borsodchem, up 3.9%, although volumes weren't large.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IN PRAGUE, there is currently no clear direction in the market, according to local traders.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PX-D closed up 0.4% Tuesday after a day of mixed trading. The two most heavily stocks, CEZ and Cesky Telekom, closed little changed, with CEZ was down 0.1% and Cesky Telekom flat. Among other stocks, Unipetrol rallied 4%, Zentiva was up 1.4%, and Komercni Banka closed 0.9% higher. [ 09-14-05 0118ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPECIAL REPORTS \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 =The National Bank of Hungary's sale of small amounts of treasuries on the market Tuesday caused yields to surge and was interpreted by traders and analysts as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denials.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OVERNIGHT NEWS \par    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can't avoid raising natural gas prices because of record high oil prices but the government will keep subsidizing residential users consuming small amounts, state news agency MTI quoted Finance Minister Janos Veres as saying Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hungarian government has reached an agreement with the workers of state-owned airport operator Budapest Airport Rt. (BPA.YY), who had threatened to strike for improved wages and benefits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Czech chemicals company Agrofert Holding AS (AGF.YY) Monday said it may seek arbitration to settle its dispute with Polish oil refiner PKN Orlen SA (PKN.WA) on dividing assets of Unipetrol AS (BAAUNIPE.PR).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Romania is looking for ways to resume its "frozen" loan agreement with the International Monetary Fund, Finance Minister Sebastian Vladescu said Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bulgaria should speed up its preparation for E.U. entry if it wants to join the union in 2007 as scheduled, a European official said Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Vanya Dragomanovich; Dow Jones Newswires; 44 207 842 9487; vanya.dragomanovich@dowjones.com [ 09-14-05 0118ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050914e19e00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
